 — but wait, run_store is created at line 262 `run_store=PostgresHarnessRunStore(session_factory)` inline. The finalization service needs the same run_store instance (or an equivalent one — it just needs load/save on the same tables; two instances of PostgresHarnessRunStore over the same session_factory are equivalent). For elegance: create `run_store = PostgresHarnessRunStore(session_factory)` as a local and pass to both. \n\n```python\n    run_store = PostgresHarnessRunStore(session_factory)\n    finalization = PostgresFinalizationService(\n        conversation_repository=ConversationRepository(session_factory),\n        ledger=PostgresFinalizationLedger(session_factory),\n        run_store=run_store,\n        memory_formation_service=runtime.formation_service,\n    )\n    return LoopController(\n        ...\n        run_store=run_store,\n        ...\n    )\n```\n\nImport: `from app.repositories.finalization_repository import PostgresFinalizationLedger`.\n\n2. **`_http_error`**: add FinalizationFailure → 503 mapping. Import FinalizationFailure from app.agent.finalization.service (already importing PostgresFinalizationService from there).\n\n```python\n    if isinstance(exc, FinalizationFailure):\n        return HTTPException(\n            status_code=503,\n            detail=f\"收口尚未完成，请稍后通过 reconcile 恢复: {exc}\",\n        )\n```\n\nPlacement: before the ValueError check (FinalizationFailure is not a ValueError — it's Exception; but put it early for clarity).\n\n3. **`_finish_unhandled_start_failure`**: must NOT downgrade a running/finalization run to FAILED — because FinalizationFailure leaves the run in running/finalization intentionally, and this callback is invoked for ANY exception from controller.start() (including FinalizationFailure). Current logic: for non-terminal, non-waiting status, it force-transitions to FAILED and saves, then finish_turn as failed. That would destroy the recoverable state and write a false \"任务启动失败\" message.\n\nRedesign: this helper's purpose is \"收口控制器启动前后未被自身捕获的异常，释放会话占用\". The distinction:\n- If the run is in running/finalization (i.e., finalize started, ledger exists) → the exception is FinalizationFailure (or something during the running/finalization window) → DO NOT touch state, DO NOT finish_turn. Reconcile owns recovery.\n- If the run is in an early phase (before finalization started) → legacy behavior: force FAILED + finish_turn. \n\nWait, but is the legacy behavior still right? Per the new architecture, any crash before finalization → run state stays e.g. running/execute_tool. `_run_guarded` catches generic exceptions and calls _finalize(FAILED) itself. `_finish_unhandled_start_failure` only runs for exceptions that ESCAPE _run_guarded — e.g.:\n- FinalizationFailure from _run_guarded's except blocks (finalize itself failed).\n- Exceptions during `run_store.create` in start() (before _run_guarded).\n- Exceptions from _prepare_interruption_state itself failing.\n- HarnessDeadlineExceeded handling: _finalize raising FinalizationFailure.\n\nSo when does this helper see a non-terminal, non-waiting status that ISN'T running/finalization? If run_store.create failed → load raises HarnessPersistenceError → handled (pass, then finish_turn — hmm, but the turn may not exist either; finish_turn returns False if turn missing — wait, no: finish_turn returns False when turn/conversation missing. But start_turn may not have been called yet! Actually in run_harness, start_turn is called BEFORE controller.start, so the turn exists. In the stream variant, start_turn is inside operation()... and cleanup runs on operation failure. If start_turn itself failed, finish_turn → turn is None → returns False → no-op. Fine.)\n\nIf _prepare_interruption_state fails (e.g., DB down), state stays in its previous phase (running/plan etc.) → helper force-FAILED + finish_turn. That's the startup/pre-finalization failure path — appropriate to keep.\n\nSo the fix: distinguish `running/finalization` (with terminal_intent) from other running phases. If status is RUNNING and phase is FINALIZATION → recovery belongs to reconcile → skip the force-fail and skip finish_turn. \n\nThe current condition structure:\n```python\nif current_status is HarnessStatus.WAITING_CONFIRMATION: should_finish_turn = False\nelif current_status in terminal_statuses: should_finish_turn = False\nelse: force FAILED...\n```\n\nNew:\n```python\nif current_status is HarnessStatus.WAITING_CONFIRMATION:\n    should_finish_turn = False\nelif current_status in terminal_statuses:\n    should_finish_turn = False\nelif phase is FINALIZATION:\n    # FinalizationFailure 把运行留在 running/finalization 等待对账；\n    # 这里降级成 FAILED 会覆盖账本锁定的收口内容。\n    should_finish_turn = False\nelse:\n    force FAILED...\n```\n\nNeed the phase: `LoopPhase(state[\"harness\"][\"phase\"])`.\n\nHmm — but one wrinkle: what if the crash happened between the controller's running/finalization save and ledger.prepare? Then state is running/finalization but NO ledger exists. Reconcile would 409 (no ledger). The run would be stuck in running/finalization forever with no recovery path. Edge case: crash window is tiny (two awaits). Options: (a) accept it — operator intervention; (b) helper could check ledger existence and force-fail only when ledger is missing. Option (b) is actually precise and not \"unreliable judgment\": the ledger is the source of truth for whether finalization has begun. \n\nLet me think about which is truly correct per the architecture:\n- Ledger exists (any stage) → finalization owns the outcome → never force-fail; reconcile recovers.\n- No ledger + running/finalization → finalize was never entered (or prepare failed). The run's final answer was never locked. Force-fail + finish_turn is the only way to release the conversation. This matches the helper's original purpose (\"controller startup前后 unhandled failures\").\n\nSo the precise rule: `should_finish_turn = False` when ledger exists OR status is waiting/terminal. Implementation:\n\n```python\nelif await PostgresFinalizationLedger(session_factory).get(\n    run_id=run_ref.run_id, user_id=run_ref.user_id\n) is not None:\n    # 收口账本已存在：结果归属 reconcile，不能伪造失败终态。\n    should_finish_turn = False\n```\n\nBut wait — ordering: only check ledger when status is RUNNING and phase is finalization? The ledger only exists after prepare, which happens only in running/finalization. Checking unconditionally (elif after waiting/terminal) is simpler and correct: ledger exists → skip. But if status is running/plan AND ledger exists (impossible per protocol)... skip force-fail anyway — ledger means finalization started; trusting the ledger is right.\n\nHmm, but hold on: if status is running/plan and the DB write for prepare crashed... no, prepare creates the row only when called. Fine — unconditional ledger check is precise.\n\nBut should I also check `phase is FINALIZATION` as belt? No — \"不要进行看起来就很不靠谱的判断逻辑\": the ledger check IS the precise condition. A running/finalization state without ledger falls through to force-FAILED — correct recovery for the prepare-crash window.\n\n4. **New endpoint** `/run/reconcile`:\n\n```python\nclass HarnessReconcileRequest(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"触发一次收口对账；身份与内容全部来自服务端账本。\"\"\"\n\n    run_id: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=128)\n    user_id: str = Field(default=settings.app.default_user_id, min_length=1, max_length=128)\n\n\n@router.post(\"/run/reconcile\")\nasync def reconcile_harness(payload: HarnessReconcileRequest) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"从收口账本当前阶段恢复未完成的收口；不重新调用 Planner 或工具。\"\"\"\n    try:\n        runtime, session_factory, _ = _require_runtime()\n        finalization = PostgresFinalizationService(\n            conversation_repository=ConversationRepository(session_factory),\n            ledger=PostgresFinalizationLedger(session_factory),\n            run_store=PostgresHarnessRunStore(session_factory),\n            memory_formation_service=runtime.formation_service,\n        )\n        result = await finalization.reconcile(\n            run_id=payload.run_id, user_id=payload.user_id\n        )\n        return _serialize_result(...)? \n```\n\nHmm — what should reconcile return? FinalizationResult is not a LoopResult. _serialize_result expects LoopResult. Return the FinalizationResult model dump:\n\n```python\n        return result.model_dump(mode=\"json\")\n```\n\nThat's fine — a plain dict response. \n\nShould reconcile build the full controller? No — reconcile only needs the finalization service (it must NOT re-enter planner/tools). Building only PostgresFinalizationService is the cleanest expression of the red line.\n\nAlso — the D-slice `_build_controller` needs the ledger import. And `HarnessReconcileRequest` in `__all__`.\n\nOne more consideration for `run_harness` (non-stream): `except Exception: await _finish_unhandled_start_failure(...); raise` — with FinalizationFailure now propagating, _finish_unhandled_start_failure must skip (ledger check handles it), then `raise` → outer `except Exception as exc: raise _http_error(exc)` → 503 via new mapping. \n\nFor the stream variant: FinalizationFailure from operation() → run_operation's `except Exception` catches it → calls on_operation_failure (cleanup → ledger check → skip) → emits stream.failed with generic message. Hmm — should the SSE surface the FinalizationFailure distinctly? The stream.failed payload says \"请通过状态接口查询最终状态\" — client can query status, see running/finalization, and call reconcile. That's acceptable; the stream contract shouldn't change. But maybe include the stage info in the payload? Keep minimal — the 503 mapping covers the REST path. Actually, let me reconsider: for the stream path, the client sees stream.failed and polls status. Status remains running/finalization. Then the client (or operator) calls reconcile. Fine.\n\nWait — one more thing about `_finish_unhandled_start_failure`: its finish_turn fallback writes status=\"failed\" assistant message. If run is in running/finalization WITH ledger → skipped entirely (should_finish_turn=False → early return). Good — history writing belongs to the ledger flow now.\n\nAlso check: does the helper need to preserve its force-FAILED logic for non-finalization statuses? Yes — kept.\n\nNow write the edits:\n\nEdit A — import PostgresFinalizationLedger + FinalizationFailure:\n```python\nfrom app.agent.finalization.service import (\n    FinalizationFailure,\n    PostgresFinalizationService,\n)\n```\nand\n```python\nfrom app.repositories.finalization_repository import PostgresFinalizationLedger\n```\n(placed alphabetically: after harness_run_repository import? Order: finalization_repository < harness_action_repository < harness_artifact_repository < harness_run_repository. So insert before harness_action_repository.)\n\nEdit B — _build_controller run_store local.\n\nEdit C — _http_error FinalizationFailure → 503.\n\nEdit D — _finish_unhandled_start_failure ledger check.\n\nEdit E — HarnessReconcileRequest model + reconcile endpoint + __all__.\n\nFor Edit D placement — the elif chain:\n\n```python\n        if current_status is HarnessStatus.WAITING_CONFIRMATION:\n            # 恢复请求可能只是 confirmation_id 或条件字段校验失败，\n            # 此时必须保留 waiting 状态，允许用户重新提交正确回复。\n            should_finish_turn = False\n        elif current_status in terminal_statuses:\n            should_finish_turn = False\n        elif await PostgresFinalizationLedger(session_factory).get(\n            run_id=run_ref.run_id, user_id=run_ref.user_id\n        ) is not None:\n            # 收口账本已锁定结果归属；运行现场保持 running/finalization，\n            # 由 reconcile 恢复，这里伪造失败终态会覆盖锁定的收口内容。\n            should_finish_turn = False\n        else:\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm wait — the ledger.get call needs to be inside the try (DB errors → caught by except Exception → logged). Yes it's inside the try block.\n\nLet me now apply the edits.","startTime":1791428023739,"lastModifiedTime":1791428023739},{"id":"18ae9925-5369-428a-b027-e032aa280274","type":"TEXT","end":true,"content":"Now 