zed but errors submodule fully loads). Then controller.py finishes, loop_controller __init__ finishes, service.py continues importing contracts — fine. No ImportError. \n\n- And service.py imports FinalizationFailure from `.errors` — natural home.\n\nSo: create `app/agent/finalization/errors.py`, keep service importing from it, controller imports from it. Also revert controller's FinalizationFailure import to... it currently imports from `app.agent.finalization.service`. Change to `app.agent.finalization.errors`. The router also imports FinalizationFailure from service — change to errors.\n\nActually — should the package __init__ also export FinalizationFailure? Keep __all__ = [\"FinalizationFailure\", \"PostgresFinalizationService\"] in __init__? The eager __init__ import of service keeps the same cycle when finalization package is imported FIRST (init → service → loop_controller init → controller → finalization.errors → fine as traced above — actually that worked in my trace). Let me verify the trace once more for entry `import app.agent.finalization` (init first):\n1. init starts → import service (line 3).\n2. service → import loop_controller.contracts → init loop_controller → import controller.\n3. controller → import finalization.errors → errors.py has no internal deps → loads fully. ✓\n4. controller finishes → LoopController defined → loop_controller init done.\n5. service continues → defines classes → done.\n6. init done. ✓\n\nAnd entry `import app.agent.loop_controller` first:\n1. init → controller → import finalization.service? NO — controller imports finalization.errors only now. errors loads. ✓ controller finishes.\n2. loop_controller done. Later service imports work fine.\n\nThe cycle is broken because errors.py is dependency-free. \n\nAlso service.py no longer needs to define FinalizationFailure — move it to errors.py. service.py imports it. `__all__` of service keeps PostgresFinalizationService; errors.py exports FinalizationFailure.\n\nUpdate:\n- New 