at the end. Exactly the user's complaint.\n\nSo the fix design is clear now. Two approaches:\n\n**方案 A（前端为主）**: frontend derives lifecycle status from event TYPE (since type semantics are already encoded: `.started` → running, `.completed` → success, `.failed`/`error` → failed). This requires a type→status mapping table in the frontend. No backend change. The events already exist for every stage transition (context.started/completed, planner.started/completed/failed, tool.started/completed/failed/retrying, action.committed, confirmation.required/resolved, run.*).\n\n**方案 B（后端为主）**: controller._emit adds explicit `status` to payload for lifecycle events (started→running, completed→success, failed→failed). More contract-explicit; frontend stays as-is (it already reads status).\n\nWhich is better? The user's style directives: elegant, follow existing patterns, no unnecessary complexity. The event TYPE already encodes lifecycle (\"x.started\"/\"x.completed\"/\"x.failed\" naming convention) — deriving status from type in the frontend is a pure presentation concern, no API change, no new payload fields, works for historical traces already saved (old traces get correct statuses retroactively!). Option B only fixes new runs and duplicates information already present in the type name.\n\nAlso need: `context.context_compiled`, `context.memory_retrieved`, `context.knowledge_retrieved`, `tool.progress`, `planner.retrying`, `analysis_task_phase` are PROGRESS events within a stage — they should NOT flip module status to success. Mapping:\n\n- `*.started` → running\n- `*.completed` → success (context.completed, planner.completed, tool.completed)\n- `*.failed`, `error`, `run.timeout`, `run.cancelled` → failed\n- `run.result` → per its payload status (completed/cancelled/timeout/failed...)\n- `*.retrying`, `tool.progress`, `context.memory_retrieved`, `context.knowledge_retrieved`, `context.context_compiled` → running (still in progress) — actually these shouldn't override a previous... hmm, in sequence: context.started(running) → memory_retrieved → knowledge_retrieved → context_compiled → context.completed(success). If progress events map to \"running\", sequence stays running until completed. Fine.\n- `action.committed` → success of the 提交动作 module (module = single event; started? There's no action.started — only committed. So the 提交动作 module only exists after the fact; status success. Before that, module absent. OK.)\n- `confirmation.required` → the 等待用户确认 module — status \"running\"? It's waiting. Pause: when confirmation.required arrives, the run pauses. Module status could be \"running\" (waiting) or a dedicated \"waiting\" — keep simple: running.\n- `confirmation.resolved` → success.\n- `run.started` → running; `run.completed` → success; run.failed → failed.\n\nWhere to implement: in `statusForEvents`, add type-based derivation when `event.status` is absent. Current code:\n\n```ts\n} else if (event.status) {\n  latestStatus = event.status;\n} else if (RESULT_EVENT_TYPES.has(event.type)) {\n  latestStatus = \"success\";\n}\n```\n\nNew: \n```ts\n} else if (event.status) {\n  latestStatus = event.status;\n} else {\n  const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n  if (derived) latestStatus = derived;\n}\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```ts\nconst FAILED_EVENT_TYPES = new Set([\"error\", \"run.timeout\", \"run.cancelled\"]); // plus .failed suffix already checked\nconst RUNNING_EVENT_TYPES = new Set([...]);\nconst SUCCESS_EVENT_TYPES = new Set([...]);\n\nfunction statusFromEventType(type: string): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (type === \"error\" || type.endsWith(\".failed\") || type === \"run.timeout\" || type === \"run.cancelled\") return \"failed\";\n  if (type === \"run.completed\" || type.endsWith(\".completed\") || type === \"action.committed\" || RESULT_EVENT_TYPES.has(type)) return \"success\";\n  if (type === \"run.started\" || type.endsWith(\".started\") || type.endsWith(\".retrying\") || type === \"tool.progress\" || type === \"confirmation.required\" || type === \"confirmation.resolved\"...) return \"running\";\n  return undefined;\n}\n```\n\nWait — confirmation.resolved → success? The 等待用户确认 module: required → waiting/running, resolved → success (确认已处理). Yes.\n\nHmm — careful with progress events overriding: sequence order matters. context.started(running) → ... → context.completed(success). Last terminal wins. With retrying: planner.started(running) → planner.failed(failed) → planner.retrying(running) → planner.completed(success). Sequence handles it. \n\nBut one subtlety: `.started` of a LATER iteration re-marks an earlier completed module back to running — that's CORRECT behavior (re-entry: iteration 2's context rebuild shows running again, then success again). Good — matches user expectation.\n\n**Issue 1: pending \"?\" icon → spinner.** After the type-based fix, a module only shows \"pending\" when NO events exist yet (e.g., step groups created by... actually groups only exist if events exist! buildStepGroups groups existing events. So when does \"pending\" appear? `buildTaskGroups` steps with fallback task.status=\"pending\" (analysis tasks before start), and nodeGroups with fallback. For main steps: group exists only with events → with the fix it'll be running/success/failed, never pending. For analysis TASKS: task created via analysis_plan → status \"pending\" until its events arrive → icon shows for pending tasks. User wants spinner instead of \"?\" for pending.\n\nHmm — but is spinner for pending right? A task that hasn't started yet showing a spinner is a bit misleading, but the user explicitly asked: \"现在pending状态的icon是一个问号，也改为转圈圈吧\" — they want the spinner. OK — change StatusIcon: pending → Loader2 spinner but maybe grey/blue distinction? User said \"也改为转圈圈\" — make it spinner. I'd do: pending → spinner in slate-400 (subtle, \"queued/starting\"), running → spinner blue-500 animate-spin. Or both identical blue. Simplest per user: pending → same Loader2 spin, maybe text-slate-400 without pulse or with. I'll propose: pending = 转圈（灰色，表示排队/即将开始），running = 转圈（蓝色）。Actually the user may simply want consistent spinning. I'll go with grey spinner for pending to keep a visual distinction, and note it in the plan (they can ask for identical).\n\nHmm wait — actually let me reconsider: keep it minimal per request — \"也改为转圈圈\" → just change the icon to the spinner. I'll use Loader2 animate-spin text-slate-400 for pending (spinning but grey). If they want blue, trivial change.\n\n**Issue 3: 全部事件 tab.** Already exists (\"工具与事件\" = AllEvents). Keep. For \"判断出具体哪个最小节点出错\": with type-based failed derivation, error events turn nodes red at every level (nodeGroups uses statusForEvents per node). Also errors carry node/action_id in payload. Maybe add: in AllEvents, failed events already get rose XCircle icon (EventDetails uses status failed for type error). Good enough. I could also surface a \"失败\" filter... keep scope: ensure failed propagation works, tab stays.\n\n**Issue 2 also mentions**: \"第一轮的上下文构建完成后，状态没有改变\" — fixed by type-based success (context.completed → success). \"然后最后都执行完后，又开始变化为成功\" — the runCompleted blanket stays as a safety net but now per-step statuses are already correct before the end. Actually should I remove the blanket `if (runCompleted...)`? Keep it as fallback for robustness (e.g., missing context.completed due to a crash) — but it can mask a module that silently never completed. With type-derivation, blanket only applies when latestStatus is still pending/running... Current: `if (runCompleted && events.length && latestStatus !== \"failed\") return \"success\"`. This overrides \"running\"→success at end (good for modules whose completed event was missed, e.g. cancelled runs → hmm, for run.cancelled the blanket doesn't apply since... runCompleted checks only run.completed type. Fine, keep.)\n\nOne more thing: node-level status (节点: context_engine etc.) — nodeGroups already uses statusForEvents per node events, so the same fix propagates to sub