nt.type === \"error\"\n      || event.type.endsWith(\".failed\")\n      || event.type === \"run.timeout\"\n      || event.type === \"run.cancelled\"\n    ) {\n      latestStatus = \"failed\";\n    } else if (event.status) {\n      latestStatus = event.status;\n    } else if (RESULT_EVENT_TYPES.has(event.type)) {\n      latestStatus = \"success\";\n    }\n  }\n  if (runCompleted && events.length && latestStatus !== \"failed\") return \"success\";\n  return latestStatus || \"pending\";\n}\n```\n\nNew design — add a derivation function near the other helpers (after statusOf / terminal, around line 113-128):\n\n```ts\n/** Harness 事件名自带生命周期语义；没有显式 status 时按事件类型推导。 */\nfunction statusFromEventType(type: string): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (type === \"error\" || type.endsWith(\".failed\") || type === \"run.timeout\" || type === \"run.cancelled\") {\n    return \"failed\";\n  }\n  if (type.endsWith(\".completed\") || type === \"action.committed\" || type === \"confirmation.resolved\" || type === \"run.completed\") {\n    return \"success\";\n  }\n  if (\n    type.endsWith(\".started\")\n    || type.endsWith(\".retrying\")\n    || type === \"tool.progress\"\n    || type === \"confirmation.required\"\n    || CONTEXT_PROGRESS_EVENT_TYPES.has(type)\n  ) {\n    return \"running\";\n  }\n  return undefined;\n}\n```\n\nWait — but the failed branch is already handled in statusForEvents first condition (error/.failed/run.timeout/run.cancelled → failed). To avoid duplication, keep statusForEvents structure and only add derivation in the else-if chain. Actually cleaner: rewrite statusForEvents to use the single derivation function:\n\n```ts\nfunction statusForEvents(events: DebugEvent[], fallback?: RunStatus, runCompleted = false): RunStatus {\n  let latestStatus = fallback;\n  for (const event of events.slice().sort((left, right) => eventOrder(left) - eventOrder(right))) {\n    if (event.status) {\n      latestStatus = event.status;\n    } else {\n      const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n      if (derived) latestStatus = derived;\n    }\n  }\n  if (runCompleted && events.length && latestStatus !== \"failed\") return \"success\";\n  return latestStatus || \"pending\";\n}\n```\n\nBut careful about precedence: previously explicit failed-check came BEFORE event.status. Could an event have type \".failed\" AND a status like \"success\"? The old code forced failed for `.failed` types regardless of status. My rewrite would let event.status override. Hmm — e.g. `tool.failed` with status? Backend tool.failed emitted by tool runtime — does it carry status=\"failed\"? If it carried status=\"failed\" anyway, no difference. To preserve exact old semantics (failed types always win), keep the failed check first, then status, then derive:\n\n```ts\nfor (...) {\n  const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n  if (derived === \"failed\") {\n    latestStatus = \"failed\";\n  } else if (event.status) {\n    latestStatus = event.status;\n  } else if (derived) {\n    latestStatus = derived;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, that's slightly convoluted. Alternative: keep the original structure and put derivation only in the final else:\n\n```ts\nfor (const event of ...) {\n  if (\n    event.type === \"error\"\n    || event.type.endsWith(\".failed\")\n    || event.type === \"run.timeout\"\n    || event.type === \"run.cancelled\"\n  ) {\n    latestStatus = \"failed\";\n  } else if (event.status) {\n    latestStatus = event.status;\n  } else {\n    const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n    if (derived) latestStatus = derived;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd statusFromEventType then only needs running/success (no failed branch since the first condition catches those):\n\nWait but \"run.cancelled\"/\"run.timeout\" are in first branch. OK so statusFromEventType without failed:\n\n```ts\nconst CONTEXT_PROGRESS_EVENT_TYPES = new Set([\n  \"context.memory_retrieved\",\n  \"context.knowledge_retrieved\",\n  \"context.context_compiled\",\n]);\n\n/** Harness 事件名自带生命周期语义；缺少显式 status 时按类型推导展示状态。 */\nfunction statusFromEventType(type: string): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (type.endsWith(\".completed\") || type === \"action.committed\" || type === \"confirmation.resolved\") {\n    return \"success\";\n  }\n  if (\n    type.endsWith(\".started\")\n    || type.endsWith(\".retrying\")\n    || type === \"tool.progress\"\n    || type === \"confirmation.required\"\n    || CONTEXT_PROGRESS_EVENT_TYPES.has(type)\n  ) {\n    return \"running\";\n  }\n  return undefined;\n}\n```\n\nNote: \"run.completed\" endsWith \".completed\" ✓. \"run.started\" endsWith \".started\" ✓. \"planner.retrying\"/\"tool.retrying\" ✓. RESULT_EVENT_TYPES branch: previously `else if (RESULT_EVENT_TYPES.has(event.type)) latestStatus = \"success\"` — my else branch derivation must preserve that. RESULT_EVENT_TYPES = reasoning_result, llm_result, analysis_task_resolved, analysis_task_result, report_plan_result, rendered_report. None of these end with .completed/.started etc. So I must keep the RESULT_EVENT_TYPES check. Merge:\n\n```ts\n  } else if (RESULT_EVENT_TYPES.has(event.type) || (derived = ...)) \n```\n\nCleaner:\n\n```ts\n    } else {\n      const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n      if (RESULT_EVENT_TYPES.has(event.type)) {\n        latestStatus = \"success\";\n      } else if (derived) {\n        latestStatus = derived;\n      }\n    }\n```\n\nHmm, or simply add RESULT_EVENT_TYPES into the success line of statusFromEventType? But RESULT_EVENT_TYPES is also used elsewhere? grep showed only line 244 usage and def at 96. Actually displayEvents uses hasReasoningResult etc. separately. Let me just include them in statusFromEventType success branch via the set:\n\n```ts\nfunction statusFromEventType(type: string): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (type.endsWith(\".completed\") || type === \"action.committed\" || type === \"confirmation.resolved\" || RESULT_EVENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n    return \"success\";\n  }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThen statusForEvents becomes:\n\n```ts\nfunction statusForEvents(events: DebugEvent[], fallback?: RunStatus, runCompleted = false): RunStatus {\n  let latestStatus = fallback;\n  for (const event of events.slice().sort((left, right) => eventOrder(left) - eventOrder(right))) {\n    if (\n      event.type === \"error\"\n      || event.type.endsWith(\".failed\")\n      || event.type === \"run.timeout\"\n      || event.type === \"run.cancelled\"\n    ) {\n      latestStatus = \"failed\";\n    } else if (event.status) {\n      latestStatus = event.status;\n    } else {\n      const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n      if (derived) latestStatus = derived;\n    }\n  }\n  if (runCompleted && events.length && latestStatus !== \"failed\") return \"success\";\n  return latestStatus || \"pending\";\n}\n```\n\nThis preserves: failed forced first, explicit status second, derivation third. And RESULT_EVENT_TYPES handled inside derivation. \n\nEdge: \"run.result\" — ends with neither; falls through → undefined → keeps previous. In StepGroup \"运行结果\" (STEP_ORDER has 运行结果), run.result event group status: no derivation → fallback/pending → runCompleted blanket turns success (run.result and run.completed both arrive at end — runCompleted true → success). Fine, unchanged behavior.\n\nEdge: `statusForEvents(stepEvents, task.status)` in buildTaskGroups passes fallback=task.status — preserved.\n\nEdge concern — the \"pending\" derivation for nodeGroups fallback: nodeGroups passes fallback (task.status or undefined). OK.\n\nOne more important check: the FAILED first-branch — `analysis_task_result` with status failed handled via taskStatus separately. OK.\n\nNow — one subtle regression risk: `.started` mapping to running means a module that NEVER completes (e.g., stream dies mid-run) stays \"running\" (spinning) instead of pending \"?\" in historical traces. For cancelled/timeout runs, run.cancelled/run.timeout events mark... they're global events (step \"开始执行\"? run.timeout type → which step? harnessStep