"running\" until the very end. Once context starts, the start phase is actually done.\n\nTwo fixes:\n\n**Fix 1 (backend, primary)**: emit `context.completed` at the end of `_build_context` — symmetric with `planner.started/completed`. This makes the module flip green naturally. It also benefits historical traces going forward.\n\n**Fix 2 (frontend)**: `开始执行` module — run.started alone. Options:\n- (a) Treat `run.started` as success for that group? No — at the moment it arrives, running is right; it should flip when the next module starts.\n- (b) Since modules only exist when they have events, \"开始执行\" group will exist from the very first event. The natural semantic: 开始执行 is complete once any OTHER module has started. Implement in buildStepGroups/ProgressView: after computing groups, if any group other than 开始执行 has events, mark 开始执行 success. Hmm — special case.\n\nAlternative (b2): map `run.started` → success directly? Then at the very first instant (before context.started arrives, which is sub-second) it would flash green then... no wait, it'd stay green — actually is that wrong? \"开始执行 = 运行开始\" — as a module \"starting the run\", once the run has started and moved on, it IS complete. At the instant run.started is the only event, showing green is... acceptable? Hmm, but the run literally just started; showing green \"done\" for 开始执行 while 构建上下文 spins is actually fine semantically (\"启动\"这一步已完成). Actually yes! Think of it as: the start phase completed when the run began executing. The spinner at start was just \"run is starting\". Showing green immediately is honest — the start action completed.\n\nHmm, but wait: there's a subtlety — the explicit event.status=\"running\" (from payload) takes priority over derivation in my statusForEvents. So mapping run.started → success via derivation won't apply because explicit status wins. I'd need to remove the payload status from run.started emission OR let derivation override. Careful: the explicit payload.status=\"running\" 