ult dimension 1024, supports 1024/768/512 etc. text-embedding-v4: 2048 default? Let me recall: \n     - text-embedding-v1: 1536 dims\n     - text-embedding-v2: 1536 dims\n     - text-embedding-v3: default 1024, supports 1024, 768, 512, 256, 128, 64 (via dimensions param)\n     - text-embedding-v4: default 1024? Actually v4 supports dimensions 2048/1536/1024/768/512/256/128/64, default 1024.\n   - To avoid rebuilding Qdrant collections, choose a model+dimension that outputs **1024** to match `vector_size: 1024`. text-embedding-v3 with default (or explicit `dimensions=1024`) or v4 with dimensions=1024. That way NO Qdrant reindexing needed. However — embeddings from a different model are NOT comparable with existing vectors! Even with same dimension, the existing vectors in Qdrant were produced by bge-large-zh. Mixing query vectors (qwen) with stored vectors (bge) is meaningless — similarity would be garbage. So **existing collections must be re-embedded** regardless of dimension match. That means: after switching, we need to re-run the metadata semantic indexing (meta_tables_semantic, meta_columns_semantic, meta_metrics_semantic, meta_dimension_values_semantic collections) and memory collections would also be affected (agent_memory_* — old memory vectors also bge-based).\n   \n   So the plan must include a re-index/rebuild step. Is there an existing rebuild script? Earlier config comment: \"应用只访问 alias；重建时先写新的物理索引，再原子切换 alias\" — that's for ES dimension values. For Qdrant semantic collections, there's probably an admin script or router for rebuilding metadata embeddings. Let me check for existing rebuild/指数 scripts (app/api/routers/semantic? there was a frontend api/semantic route in .next types). There's a `semantic-upload-review.tsx` frontend component — semantic data upload and review → suggests there's an admin flow to rebuild metadata embeddings from the admin UI. So re-indexing metadata may be as simple as re-uploading/re-embedding via the existing semantic pipeline.\n\n3. **Design decision: how to wire Qianwen embedding**:\n   Option A: `OpenAIEmbeddings` from langchain_openai with base_url=dashscope compatible-mode, model=text-embedding-v3 (or v4), dimensions=1024, api_key from env. Config: extend embedding section with `provider: dashscope`, `model_name`, `api_key` env, `dimensions`. The EmbeddingClientManager chooses backend by provider.\n   - OpenAIEmbeddings supports `dimensions` param (for models that support it) and `timeout` via httpx client config. Also `chunk_size` for batching (DashScope batch limit: text-embedding-v3 batch up to 10 texts per request for v3? Actually OpenAI-compatible embeddings on DashScope: batch size limit 25 for v1/v2, 10 for v3? Need care: set chunk_size small, e.g., 6 or 10, and check `max_retries`).\n   - OpenAIEmbeddings also supports `timeout` (request_timeout) — aligns with the hard-timeout work we just did.\n   \n   Option B: Use DashScope native SDK (dashscope package, TextEmbedding.call). More dependency, less aligned with existing LangChain usage. Reject.\n\n   Option A is clearly better given they already use the same API key/base_url.\n\n4. **Keep the timeout subclass pattern**: OpenAIEmbeddings has `request_timeout` param built in — no need for custom wait_for wrapper, but could still wrap. OpenAIEmbeddings accepts `timeout` param (v0.2+: `request_timeout` deprecated alias). We can pass `timeout=30`.\n\n5. **Rate limits/cost**: DashScope embedding is cheap; QPS limits for free tier... batch size and concurrency: metadata_recall max_concurrent_terms: 4 concurrent searches — fine.\n\n6. **Migration plan**:\n   - config.yaml: change embedding section: provider/model_name/api_key env/base_url/dimensions/batch_size/timeout_seconds. Keep local TEI as fallback option (provider: local) so they can switch back.\n   - `EmbeddingClientManager.init()`: branch on provider → OpenAIEmbeddings(...) or existing HuggingFaceEndpointEmbeddings. The TimeoutHuggingFaceEmbeddings stays for local; for