\nActually wait — let me reconsider using payload.iteration directly: makeEvent could capture `iteration` (from event.iteration ?? inferred). But bridged tool.progress events would then need the running round — which requires tracking \"current round\" while appending events sequentially. appendExecutionEvent processes events in arrival order, so a module-level \"current round\" counter works: on each context.started, round++. Assign every incoming event the current round. This handles everything uniformly, including replayed history (trace API replays in order).\n\nHmm, but one nuance: for history loading, events come from DB in sequence order — same inference works.\n\nSo my recommendation:\n- 前端在 appendExecutionEvent/归一化时维护当前轮次：遇到 `context.started` 轮次 +1；每个事件记下 `round`。\n- 步骤返回 tab 顶层改为按轮次分组：`第 1 轮`、`第 2 轮`…，轮内按现有 step 顺序（构建上下文 → 任务划分 → 执行工具）展示；跨轮的固定环节（开始执行、最终收口/运行结果）单独放在最前/最后（或按归属轮次）。\n- 不需要后端改动（虽然事件已带 iteration，但 tool.progress 桥接事件没有，统一用边界推断最简单一致）。\n\nWait — should I double check whether tool.progress events really lack iteration? The tool runtime bridges query_graph custom events via event_writer.custom(payload) — payload is the query graph's value dict; unlikely to include harness iteration. And tool.started/tool.completed emitted by controller? Actually tool events are emitted by ToolRuntime (runtime.py _publish_result → writes ToolResult + emits?). The controller emits tool events? Let me not over-verify; boundary inference works regardless.\n\nAlso — the 进度 tab (ProgressView) — does it need the same treatment? The user's complaint is about 步骤返回 tab. ProgressView shows modules by node with real-time status (my earlier work). Keep that as is for now; mention optionally.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider: does the user want me to implement now, or discuss? \"你感觉呢？\" — they're asking my opinion on their analysis (\"应该是第二轮\"). I should:\n1. Confirm their reading is correct (timeline: round 1 context 12:20:15-33 → plan 12:20:33-41 → query_data ~12:20:41-12:2