具. I can join step labels with \" → \". \n\n3. **进度 tab latest-wins**:\n   - Current ProgressView groups by `event.step` and computes status via statusForEvents (failed-first).\n   - Change: module status should be determined by the module's LATEST round. I.e., partition events by iteration, take the max iteration partition per step... wait, but for a step like 构建上下文, round 2 events are its latest. For 执行工具, round 1 might have tool events, round 2 none → latest = round 1 (success). For a step with failure in round 1 and success in round 2 (retry), latest round events → success. \n   - Implementation: in ProgressView, for each step group, compute `latestRound = max(iteration of events)`, then status = statusForEvents(events of that round only). Hmm — but careful with run-level events (run.completed in last round): step \"运行结果\" only exists in last round anyway.\n   - Wait, simpler and equivalent: status = statusForEvents(events where iteration === maxIterationOfThisStep). Since events are sorted by sequence, \"latest round\" = last round. \n   - Actually even simpler: the failed-first logic in statusForEvents stays, but we feed it only the latest round's events. Within the latest round, failed-first still applies correctly (e.g. planner.failed then planner.retrying within same round... hmm, retry within the same round: planner.retrying then planner.completed — statusForEvents processes in sequence order, final status = completed → success. Good. And a tool.failed followed by tool.retrying followed by tool.completed in same round → success. Good — failed-first isn't \"first failure wins\", it's \"last relevant event wins\" actually! Let me re-read statusForEvents:\n\n```js\nfor (const event of events sorted by sequence) {\n  if (error/failed/timeout/cancelled) latestStatus = \"failed\";\n  else if (event.status) latestStatus = event.status;\n  else { derived; if (derived) latestStatus = derived; }\n}\n```\n\nThis iterates in order and overwrites — so the LAST event determines status! It's NOT failed-first — it's latest-event-wins already! Wait, the summary said \"failed check first\" — but looking at the actual code: the if/else chain per event, in sequence order. A failed event sets failed, but a LATER success event overwrites it. So within a step, round-1 failure then round-2 success → success already! \n\nHmm wait — but then why did I describe \"failed check first\"? Let me re-read... `statusForEvents(events, fallback, runCompleted)`: iterates sorted events; each event updates latestStatus. Last event wins. So the \"any failure sticks\" claim I made earlier was WRONG?\n\nHold on. Let me think about the actual scenario: query_data round 1: tool.started (running) → tool.failed (failed). Round 2: context... (different step) → planner... → tool.started (running) → tool.completed (success). The 执行工具 step group contains all tool.* events across rounds: last one is tool.completed → success. So the module shows success already!\n\nHmm, so was my earlier claim wrong? The module status in ProgressView: `statusForEvents(currentEvents(values), undefined, runCompleted)`. currentEvents filters out stale \"running\" events (superseded ones). The last-event-wins logic... For the interrupted case there's special handling. \n\nWait — but there's a subtlety: `runCompleted && events.length && latestStatus !== \"failed\"` → success. If run completed and the LAST event of the step isn't failed → success. OK.\n\nSo actually the current behavior is already \"latest event wins\" within a step group! My earlier claim (\"出过失败就永远是失败\") was incorrect? Let me double check with the screenshot from the earlier bug discussion... In the previous session I described statusForEvents as \"failed check first → explicit event.status → derived\" — that was about the per-event if/else priority (failed event TYPES force failed regardless of event.status), not about ordering across events. Right — within one event, if its type is *.failed, status = failed even if event.status says otherwise. Across events, it's sequential overwrite = latest wins.\n\nBUT there's one real gap: `currentEvents` — it removes running events that have terminalScopes. Terminal scopes are computed per eventScope (step::task::node::phase). A tool.started (running) in round 1 followed by tool.completed (terminal) in round 1 — same scope → the running one is dropped, keeping the terminal. Fine.\n\nHmm — so is the Progress tab actually already latest-wins at the STEP level? Mostly yes, via sequential overwrite. Wait, but there's a catch: `statusFromEventType` for tool.progress returns \"running\". Round 2's context events update the 构建上下文 step: last event = context.completed → success. Good.\n\nOK so my \"latest-wins fix\" may be a no-op... EXCEPT: the special-case `if (runCompleted && latestStatus !== \"failed\") return \"success\"` — after a run completes, a step whose last round had... hmm.\n\nWait, wait. Let me recheck the actual failure scenario I claimed: \"第 1 轮 query_data 失败、第 2 轮重试成功，模块还挂着失败\". With sequential overwrite: events sorted by sequence — round 1 tool.failed (failed) ... round 2 tool.started (running) → tool.completed (success). Last = success. So module shows success. My claim was wrong!\n\nHmm, unless... `run.completed` handling: `if (runCompleted && events.length && latestStatus !== \"failed\") return \"success\"` — this forces success. Not the issue.\n\nSo actually the current implementation already behaves correctly for the multi-round case?! Then the only real Progress-tab change is the \"第 N 轮\" indicator. Good — that simplifies. I should be honest about this correction in my reply: I re-read statusForEvents and it's already sequential latest-wins (I mis-stated earlier); the only Progress change needed is the round indicator.\n\nHmm wait, one more check — the `interrupted` case and `partial`. Not relevant.\n\nBut hold on — there IS one subtle issue: the tool.progress events from round 1's query_data and round 2's query_data — compacted into one tool.progress display item? That's display-level, not status. Fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time whether latest-event-wins can be wrong for the Progress tab: scenario — round 1 tool fails, round 2 planner decides \"finish\" without re-running the tool. 执行工具 step: last event = round 1's tool.failed → failed. Correct!\n\nScenario — round 1 tool fails, round 2 planner retries tool and it succeeds: last = success. Correct.\n\nOK. So Progress tab: only add the round indicator. \n\n## Now the 步骤返回 rework in detail\n\nCurrent render in ExecutionPanel:\n```jsx\n<TabsContent value=\"returns\">\n  {steps.length ? steps.map((group) => <StepCard key={group.step} group={group} />) : <empty/>}\n</TabsContent>\n```\n\n`steps = buildStepGroups(orderedEvents, tasks)`.\n\nNew design:\n```ts\ninterface RoundGroup {\n  round: number;       // 0-based iteration\n  label: string;       // 第 1 轮\n  status: RunStatus;\n  steps: StepGroup[];\n  stepSummary: string; // 构建上下文 → 任务划分 → 执行工具\n}\n```\n\nFunction:\n```ts\nfunction buildRoundGroups(events: DebugEvent[], tasks: TaskSummary[]): { rounds: RoundGroup[]; single: boolean } \n```\n\nHmm, how to handle tasks (执行分析任务 pseudo-group)? Currently buildStepGroups appends the 执行分析任务 group if tasks exist. Analysis tasks belong to... the analyze_data tool (old graph or harness analyze tool). Their events (analysis_task_*) have taskId, filtered out of step groups (`!item.taskId`). For harness runs, analyze_data tool bridges events — do analysis_task_* events have iteration? They come via writer.custom? Actually analysis_task_phase etc. are bridged with type preserved? They come through the tool's event writer custom()... with custom_type. Hmm, complex. Keep current behavior: tasks stay a pseudo-step inside the round where they occurred, or... simpler: keep the 执行分析任务 group appended to the LAST round (or the round where the events are). \n\nSimplest v1: buildRoundGroups partitions events by iteration; for each round, call buildStepGroups(roundEvents, tasksForRound) where tasksForRound = tasks built from round events? But buildTasks uses analysis_plan events which may span... ugh.\n\nPragmatic v1: tasks pseudo-group goes into the round that contains its events. I can compute tasks from ALL events (current behavior) and attach the 执行分析任务 group to the round where the latest task event lives. OR simpler: keep tasks out of round partitioning — if tasks exist, append the 执行分析任务 StepGroup to the last round's steps. Actually currently buildStepGroups pushes it as a separate top-level group. For v1, I'll attach it to the round where most task events are... too clever. \n\nDecision: buildStepGroups keeps its signature and behavior (used per round with that round's events; tasks passed only to the round that contains task events). How to know which round: find iteration of first analysis_task_* event... The bridged analysis events (analysis_task_phase etc.) — these come through custom() with their own type? Let me check how they reach the frontend: displayLabel handles \"analysis_task_phase\" as event.type — so these arrive as distinct types, not tool.progress. They must be bridged differently (the analyze tool emits them with real types via... hmm, writer.custom forces tool.progress). Unless the analysis flow goes through a different path (the old graph streaming in harness mode?). \n\nWait — for HARNESS runs, analyze_data is a tool; its internal events bridge via custom() → tool.progress. But displayLabel has analysis_task_phase handlers — those are for OLD-graph runs (the panel predates harness). For harness runs, analysis tasks appear as tool.progress with custom payload. And buildTasks looks for `event.type === \"analysis_plan\"` — old-graph type. So for harness runs, tasks = [] probably. Fine — don't overthink tasks; keep current code path; in the rework, pass `tasks` only to the round whose events include analysis_plan (i.e., round of the plan event), else last round. Simpler: pass tasks to ALL rounds' buildStepGroups — each round would then append the 执行分析任务 pseudo group... that duplicates it across rounds. Bad.\n\nCleanest minimal: partition events by round. Compute tasks once. Determine `taskRound` = iteration of the analysis_plan event (or last round if not found). Each round calls buildStepGroups(roundEvents, round === taskRound ? tasks : []). If tasks exist but no iteration info → last round. OK.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me simplify even further. Is per-round buildStepGroups reuse right? buildStepGroups filters `!STREAM_EVENT_TYPES.has(item.type) && !item.taskId` then groups by step, sorts by orderOf, appends task group. Reusing per round = exactly the desired per-round structure. Yes.\n\nRound status: statusForEvents(round's visible events, undefined, runCompleted). runCompleted = run.completed exists globally (pass in).\n\nSingle round (all events share same iteration): skip wrapper — current rendering of StepCards directly. Multi-round: render RoundCard (a details element) containing StepCards.\n\nRoundCard design (match StepCard styling):\n```jsx\n<details open={round is last && has failure?} className=\"rounded-xl border ...\">\n  <summary>\n    <StatusIcon status={round.status}/>\n    <span>第 N 轮</span>\n    <span className=\"subtitle\">{stepSummary}</span>  // 构建上下文 → 任务划分 → 执行工具\n  </summary>\n  <div className=\"space-y-2\">{round.steps.map(<StepCard/>)}</div>\n</details>\n```\n\nSubtitle step chain: unique step labels in time order: e.g. `构建上下文 → 任务划分 → 执行工具`. From the round's steps (sorted by orderOf) — join(\" → \"). Nice and compact.\n\nopen state: open the round if it contains a failed step? StepCard already auto-opens failed steps (open={group.status === \"failed\"}). Round: open if status === \"failed\" || it's the last round && run not completed? Keep simple: open={round.status === \"failed\"}. Hmm — for the effect demo, maybe open the LAST round by default so user sees current activity? v1: open failed; otherwise collapsed. Actually, thinking about UX: with rounds collapsed by default, the 步骤返回 tab becomes a compact loop timeline — nice. But the user wants to \"看看效果\" — either way works. I'll do: open={round.status === \"failed\" || (isLastRound && !runCompleted)} — the active round stays visible while running. That's genuinely useful (live round visible). Is that a \"不靠谱判断\"? No, it's reasonable.\n\nHmm, keep it simpler: `open={round.status === \"failed\" || round.status === \"running\"}`. Running round auto-open. Same effect, simpler condition. \n\n## iteration capture in makeEvent\n\nAdd to DebugEvent: `iteration: number`. In makeEvent: `const iteration = typeof event.iteration === \"number\" ? event.iteration : 0;` — top-level. But wait: does the top-level StreamEvent from SSE have iteration? HarnessEvent.model_dump → {event_id, event_type, run_ref, phase, iteration, action_id, source, timestamp, payload}. So yes, top-level iteration. For history-loaded events — need to check trace API returns same shape. Let me check the trace/history loading path quickly (app/api — harness trace endpoint). The summary mentioned `_sse_response(event_sink, session_factory)` and trace endpoints. Let me grep for how history events are serialized.\n\nAlso `run_ref` is a nested dict; payload sanitized. The frontend `event.step` — where does step come from? HarnessEvent has no `step` field! But makeEvent reads event.step... The API layer must flatten/transform events before SSE (adding step label). Hmm — the SSE `data:` is `event.model_dump()` per format_sse. But the frontend receives step labels like \"构建上下文\"... those come from payload? displayLabel uses event.type mapping — the Chinese labels are hardcoded in displayLabel by type! And `event.step` — used as fallback. Where does step get set? Maybe the API transforms. I should check the SSE endpoint transformation (harness_api.py or similar) to confirm both `step` and `iteration` presence. Let me grep for where step is added to events for the frontend.\n\nLet me look at the API layer: grep '\"step\"' in app/api or app/agent/harness.","startTime":1791428023743,"lastModifiedTime":1791428023743},{"id":"a2568608-f2e7-4477-893d-f3d5ffca82a0","type":"TEXT","end":true,"content":"进度 tab 我重新核对了 `statusForEvents`，要先纠正我自己的一个说法：它是按 sequence 顺序逐条覆盖的（最后一条事件决定状态），所以\"失败永远挂着\"其实不成立，多轮重试后模块会正确显示成功。进度 tab 只需要加\"第 N 轮\"提示。改之前最后确认一下前端拿到的 SSE/历史事件里 `step` 和 `iteration` 的实际位置："},{"id":"call_d6eb963c8fa24ad49e2813e7","type":"TOOL","toolName":"grep_content","params":{"-n":"true","glob":"app/api/**/*.py","output_mode":"content","path":"/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis","pattern":"\"step\"|step=|iteration"},"toolState":"executed","result":{"output":"Found 3 matches\n\n/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis/app/api/routers/harness.py:\n  Line 319:         max_iterations=settings.harness.max_iterations,\n\n/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis/app/api/routers/harness.py:\n  Line 357:         \"iteration\": snapshot.iteration,\n\n/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis/app/api/routers/harness.py:\n  Line 498:                 iteration=int(getattr(result, \"iteration\", 0)),","metadata":{"pattern":"\"step\"|step=|iteration","relevantFiles":[{"path":"/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis/app/api/routers/harness.py","startLine":319,"endLine":319},{"path":"/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis/app/api/routers/harness.py","startLine":357,"endLine":357},{"path":"/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis/app/api/routers/harness.py","startLine":498,"endLine":498}]}}}]},{"id":"6aab6ffbfe8fcb022bfab649","discard":false,"metrics":{"duration":3322},"hasUnpairedToolUse":false,"children":[{"id":"call_26597de301c84f60b178ab8a","type":"TOOL","toolName":"grep_content","params":{"-n":"true","glob":"app/**/*.py","output_mode":"content","path":"/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis","pattern":"def _to_stream_event|step.*=.*event|to_frontend