ntent":"The user shows the context_engine node drawer with 5 返回项 (single round now — round grouping works). But the individual items inside (开始构建上下文, 读取记忆, 召回语义知识, 上下文已组装, 上下文构建完成) all show the empty-circle icon (undefined status), even though the drawer header shows \"success\".\n\nThe user asks: \"给我整体看一下，那些还没有成功状态\" — \"Look at this overall — those items still don't have success status.\"\n\nSo the issue: individual events inside the drawer don't show their derived status (should show success for completed lifecycle events), while the node group header correctly shows success.\n\nWhy do individual items show undefined status? In `EventDetails`:\n```tsx\n<StatusIcon status={event.status || (event.type === \"error\" ? \"failed\" : undefined)} />\n```\n\nIt uses `event.status` only — which comes from `statusOf(event.status)` in makeEvent (the raw `status` field of the SSE event). Harness lifecycle events (context.started, context.completed, etc.) don't carry an explicit `status` field — backend `_emit` doesn't pass status. So `event.status` is undefined → icon = empty circle.\n\nBut `statusFromEventType` (used in statusForEvents for GROUP status derivation) knows: `context.completed` → ends with `.completed` → success; `context.started` → ends with `.started` → running. The individual item rendering never applies this derivation!\n\nFix: in `EventDetails`, derive status from event type when explicit status is missing:\n```tsx\n<StatusIcon status={event.status || statusFromEventType(event.type) || (event.type === \"error\" ? \"failed\" : undefined)} />\n```\n\nWait — but careful: this same EventDetails is used in the 全部事件 tab too. Deriving status there — is that desirable? The 全部事件 tab shows raw event stream; giving each event its lifecycle-derived icon is consistent and helpful. The earlier session already added this derivation for GROUP status; applying it per-item makes items consistent with groups.\n\nHmm, but there's a nuance: `statusFromEventType` returns \"running\" for `.started` events — showing a spinner on 开始构建上下文 even after the round completed. Is that right for a historical view? The event itself IS a \"start\" marker — the thing it started has since completed (context.completed exists later). Showing a permanent spinner on \"开始构建上下文\" would be misleading in a completed run.\n\nBetter semantic: an event's display status should reflect what THAT event represents:\n- `.completed` events → success ✓\n- `.started` events → hmm. In a live view, spinner = in progress. In a finished run, the started event is historically \"was started (and finished)\". \n\nWhat did the earlier work decide for groups? statusForEvents: sequential overwrite — last event wins. For individual display items, the honest representation of a `.started` event item in a finished run... \n\nOption A: `.started` → success if the corresponding `.completed` exists later? That's pairing logic — complex.\n\nOption B: For individual items, treat `.started` as \"running\" while live, but that shows perpetual spinner in history — bad.\n\nOption C: `.started` → derive as \"success\" too? \"开始构建上下文\" marked success is weird semantically (\"started\" isn't \"succeeded\") — but visually in a completed run, a checkmark row is cleaner than a spinner. Hmm.\n\nWait — let me reconsider. What does the user actually want? They said \"那些还没有成功状态\" — they want the items to show success. The parent node shows success; the items inside show empty circles. The user finds it inconsistent.\n\nLook at displayLabel: `if (event.status === \"success\") return event.step + \"结果\";` etc. — the label adapts to status. For harness events displayLabel is type-based (开始构建上下文 etc.).\n\nSimplest consistent rule for items: use the same derivation as groups — `event.status || statusFromEventType(type)`. `.started` → running (spinner). In a live run that's correct. In a completed run... the spinner stays. Hmm — but wait, is that actually bad? In the 全部事件 tab, each raw event is a historical record; a spinner icon on a \"started\" record in a finished run is odd.\n\nAlternative: give EventDetails a `runCompleted`-ish context? Too invasive for v1.\n\nLet me think about what's actually most truthful and elegant:\n- context.started: represents the start of context building. The NODE (group) already shows overall status. The item-level icon should ideally show: did this phase-transition complete? For started events, if the paired completed event exists, the started event \"happened successfully\" → checkmark is fine (\"开始任务划分\" ✓ = planning did start and finish). Actually for the user, a checkmark on 开始构建上下文 in a completed run reads naturally: \"this step happened\".\n- But during a live run, 开始构建上下文 checkmark + later 上下文构建完成 checkmark — both checkmarks — loses the \"in progress\" signal? No — the IN-PROGRESS signal comes from the CURRENT phase: the latest started-without-completed pair. E.g., during context building: 开始构建上下文 (started) → if we mark started as \"running\" (spinner), the currently-executing phase shows the spinner, and completed ones show checkmarks. That's actually the PERFECT live indicator!\n\nBut the problem: after completion, the started item's icon stays a spinner forever (since its status is derived statically from type, not from whether the run moved on).\n\nHmm — BUT: `currentEvents` filters superseded running events in GROUP views! In displayEvents (used for drawer items), no filtering of running events — displayEvents only filters progress/chunk duplicates. The drawer shows ALL events including started ones.\n\nSo the elegant fix consistent with existing machinery:\n- Item status = `event.status || statusFromEventType(event.type)`\n- `.started` events show spinner... forever in history. BAD.\n\nUnless — displayEvents also applies some \"terminal scope\" logic? currentEvents drops running events when a terminal event with same scope exists. If the drawer used currentEvents, the started items would disappear from the list when completed — but user WANTS to see 开始构建上下文 in the list (it's informative).\n\nOK here's another angle — what if the item icon for `.started` events shows success when the run/group has moved past it? Simplest robust approximation: pass down whether a later event with the same type-prefix exists... complex.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's PRACTICAL and clean:\n\nThe lifecycle pairs: context.started/context.completed, planner.started/planner.completed, tool.started/tool.completed, confirmation.required/resolved. For every `.started` item, a matching `.completed` (or `.failed`) item appears later in the same node group. \n\nRule: for a `.started` event, look ahead in the same group: if a terminal counterpart exists (same prefix, .completed/.failed), show success/failed? No wait — the started item showing \"success\" means \"the start happened\", and the completed item already shows the outcome. Two checkmarks. That's fine visually — the timeline reads: ✓开始 → ✓完成. And a pending start (no counterpart yet, live) → spinner.\n\nBut implementing \"look ahead\" requires group context in EventDetails. displayEvents(events) has the full group — I can compute there: for each event, if it's a `.started`/`.required` marker and a later terminal counterpart of same prefix exists in the group, mark it success; if later `.failed` counterpart → failed? Hmm — actually simpler: the STARTED item inherits nothing; let it be success when ANY later terminal event of the same prefix exists.\n\nHmm, is this over-engineering? Let me reconsider Option C: derive `.started` → \"success\" unconditionally at item level. Live run: 开始构建上下文 shows ✓ the moment it's emitted, then 读取记忆 spinner... no wait — those CONTEXT_PROGRESS events (memory_retrieved etc.) derive \"running\" → spinner. So live: ✓开始构建上下文, ⟳读取记忆, ⟳召回语义知识, ⟳上下文已组装, then at end all ✓ except 开始构建上下文 already ✓. The \"开始\" item showing ✓ immediately is slightly premature but harmless — the actual progress signal comes from the middle items and the group icon. And completed run: everything ✓ — clean, matches user expectation (\"那些还没有成功状态\" — they want ✓).\n\nHmm, but for planner.started → success immediately... during planning (8s-24s), planner.started shows ✓ while actually in progress. The planner.completed item would show spinner during that window? No — completed event doesn't exist yet. So during planning: ✓开始任务划分, and nothing else spinning — the GROUP icon spins (planning_agent group running via statusForEvents: last event planner.started → running). Item-level: 开始任务划分 ✓ — \"planning has started\" — acceptable.\n\nvs Option B (started → running/spinner): live run shows spinner on started item — great; finished run keeps perpetual spinners on all started items — terrible.\n\nThe deciding factor: the finished-run state is what the user is looking at and complaining about. Perpetual spinners would be a worse bug than premature checkmarks. \n\nBUT — wait. Can I have both? The started item could show success only when its terminal counterpart exists ANYWHERE in the group (or run), else spinner. That's the \"look ahead\" version — best of both. How complex? In displayEvents(events), I have the group's events. Build a set of terminal prefixes: for each terminal event (.completed/.failed/tool.failed etc.), add its type prefix. Then when rendering a .started item, check if prefix in terminal set. Actually simpler: \n\n```ts\nfunction itemStatus(event: DebugEvent, events: DebugEvent[]): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (event.status) return event.status;\n  if (event.type === \"error\") return \"failed\";\n  const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n  if (derived === \"running\" && (event.type.endsWith(\".started\") || event.type === \"confirmation.required\")) {\n    // 同一生命周期已有终态事件时，started 标记视为已完成\n    const prefix = event.type.replace(/\\.(started|retrying)$/, \"\");\n    const hasTerminal = events.some((other) => other.type === prefix + \".completed\" || other.type === prefix + \".failed\");\n    if (hasTerminal) return \"success\";\n  }\n  return derived;\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait — but tool.progress events derive \"running\" — should those show spinner always? tool.progress items in a finished run: \"工具执行进度\" with spinner — same problem! In the earlier screenshot (first user message today), the items were 开始任务划分 etc. Now this screenshot shows context items. tool.progress items (执行 SQL etc. bridged from query_data) — those come in progress pairs? The bridged old-graph events: writer({\"type\": \"progress\", \"step\":..., \"status\": \"running\"}) then {\"status\": \"success\"} — these bridge as tool.progress with custom payload including status? Let me think — writer.custom(payload): value = dict(payload), custom_type = value.pop(\"type\"), node... payload={custom_type, **value} — value includes \"status\": \"running\"/\"success\"! And toHarnessStreamEvent spreads payload top-level → event.status = \"running\"/\"success\" → makeEvent statusOf(\"running\")→running, statusOf(\"success\")→success! So bridged progress events DO carry explicit status at top level. \n\nSo the items lacking status are exactly the harness lifecycle events (context.*, planner.*, action.committed, tool.started/completed, run.*) — which have no explicit status field. And those are precisely what statusFromEventType handles by type.\n\nSo the fix scope: in EventDetails, derive from type when no explicit status. And handle the .started-perpetual-spinner issue.\n\nNow — where to apply: EventDetails is used in: \n1. Drawer node items (from displayEvents) — the screenshot\n2. StepCard group.events fallback\n3. AllEvents tab\n4. TaskBody node/display items\n\nAll should get consistent derivation. The cleanest: do it in displayEvents? That only covers node-group items. AllEvents passes events directly to EventDetails without group context.\n\nDecision: implement `itemStatus(event, siblings?)`. Where siblings available (displayEvents, nodeGroups' display), pass group events for the look-ahead; AllEvents passes compacted list (also has the pairs — could pass the whole list, O(n²) with n up to 1000... .some over 1000 for each of 1000 = 1M ops — fine actually, but compacted list is already deduped).\n\nSimpler alternative that avoids look-ahead entirely: EventDetails derives `event.status || statusFromEventType(type)`, and we accept `.started` → spinner forever? No — bad.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider: is a spinner on \"开始构建上下文\" in a FINISHED run really unacceptable? Yes — it screams \"still running\" when the drawer header says success. Definitely unacceptable.\n\nSo look-ahead it is. But where's the cleanest place? Since ALL item rendering goes through EventDetails, and EventDetails doesn't know siblings... \n\nAlternative clean approach: compute item status at group-build time. displayEvents and the AllEvents path... Actually — better idea: compute it in makeEvent? No — makeEvent is sequential append; at arrival time of context.started, no completed yet; status frozen at \"running\". Later events don't retroactively update earlier items. UNLESS we recompute at render time — which group-build functions do.\n\nOK let me design concretely:\n\nIn execution-panel.tsx:\n\n```ts\nfunction lifecycleTerminalPrefixes(events: DebugEvent[]) {\n  const prefixes = new Set<string>();\n  for (const event of events) {\n    if (event.type.endsWith(\".completed\") || event.type.endsWith(\".failed\")) {\n      prefixes.add(event.type.replace(/\\.(completed|failed)$/, \"\"));\n    }\n  }\n  return prefixes;\n}\n\nfunction itemStatus(event: DebugEvent, siblings: DebugEvent[] | undefined): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (event.status) return event.status;\n  if (event.type === \"error\") return \"failed\";\n  const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n  if (siblings && (derived === \"running\" || derived === undefined) && (event.type.endsWith(\".started\") || event.type === \"confirmation.required\")) {\n    const prefix = event.type.replace(/\\.started$/, \"\");\n    if (lifecycleTerminalPrefixes(siblings).has(prefix)) return \"success\";\n  }\n  return derived;\n}\n```\n\nHmm — this is getting complicated. Let me simplify. The pairing rule: `.started` item → success if `<prefix>.completed` exists among siblings. Confirmation.required → confirmation.resolved exists → success.\n\nActually — even simpler and MORE accurate without prefix logic: a `.started` event means \"this phase was entered\". If the GROUP is terminal (success/failed), then every started marker within it concluded → show group-consistent status? No — tool.failed in a group makes group failed but a specific started may have succeeded... overcomplicating again.\n\nHonest simplicity check — user directive: \"不要进行看起来就很不靠谱的判断逻辑\" (no sketchy heuristics). The prefix-pairing rule is NOT sketchy — it's the event contract: every X.started is concluded by X.completed/X.failed (harness design). It's a lifecycle invariant, not a heuristic. OK.\n\nImplementation plan:\n1. `itemStatus(event, siblings)` as above but cleaner:\n\n```ts\n/** 事件条目的展示状态：显式 status 优先，其次按类型推导；started 标记在生命周期已有终态时按完成展示。 */\nfunction itemStatus(event: DebugEvent, siblings: DebugEvent[]): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (event.status) return event.status;\n  if (event.type === \"error\") return \"failed\";\n  if (event.type.endsWith(\".started\") || event.type === \"confirmation.required\") {\n    const prefix = event.type.replace(/\\.started$/, \"\");\n    const resolved = siblings.some((item) => item.type === `${prefix}.completed` || item.type === `${prefix}.failed`);\n    if (resolved) return \"success\";\n  }\n  return statusFromEventType(event.type);\n}\n```\n\nWait — confirmation.required prefix: \"confirmation.required\".replace(/\\.started$/) = \"confirmation.required\" → looks for confirmation.required.completed? No — the pair is confirmation.resolved. Special-case: \n\n```ts\nconst prefix = event.type === \"confirmation.required\" ? \"confirmation\" : event.type.replace(/\\.started$/, \"\");\nconst resolved = siblings.some((item) => item.type === `${prefix}.completed` || item.type === `${prefix}.failed` || (event.type === \"confirmation.required\" && item.type === \"confirmation.resolved\"));\n```\n\nGetting ugly. Hmm. Do confirmation items actually appear without status? confirmation.required/resolved — do they carry explicit status? Controller _emit doesn't pass status. So yes they'd be undefined. But the moduleNode maps confirmation.* to loop_controller node. The screenshot issue is context/planner items. Scope check: user said \"整体看一下\" — fix the general mechanism, include confirmation via a small mapping.\n\nAlternative — avoid prefix string surgery: check \"any sibling event of same lifecycle family with terminal outcome\":\n\nFamily = type up to first \".\" (context, planner, tool, confirmation, run, finalization). started item's family F: if siblings contain F.completed or F.resolved (or F.failed?) → success. But tool family: tool.started in round 1 succeeded, round 2 tool.failed exists → started shows success ✓ correct (that start DID conclude). But wait — does started→success make sense when the outcome was failed? \"工具开始执行 ✓\" while 工具执行失败 ✗ — the started itself did happen; checkmark on \"started\" is fine (it happened). Or should started show failed when its run failed? The timeline: ⟳→✗ reads better as: started ✓ (it started), failed ✗ (outcome). Both visible. Fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually — maybe simpler semantic for started items when terminal exists in family: just use... no. Keep: terminal in family → success.\n\nEdge: tool.retrying → statusFromEventType → running. After family terminal exists → still spinner? retrying item in finished run with spinner — same issue. Include .retrying in the \"started-like\" set? displayLabel has 工具执行重试. For consistency: treat .started and .retrying alike: if family has terminal → success.\n\nLet me define:\n\n```ts\nconst LIFECYCLE_IN_PROGRESS_SUFFIXES = [\".started\", \".retrying\"];\n\n/** Harness 生命周期事件的 started/retrying 标记：同族已有终态事件时视为已发生完成。 */\nfunction itemStatus(event: DebugEvent, siblings: DebugEvent[]): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (event.status) return event.status;\n  if (event.type === \"error\") return \"failed\";\n  const family = event.type.split(\".\")[0];\n  const inProgress = event.type.endsWith(\".started\") || event.type.endsWith(\".retrying\") || event.type === \"confirmation.required\";\n  if (inProgress && siblings.some((item) => item.type === `${family}.completed` || item.type === `${family}.failed` || item.type === `${family}.resolved`)) {\n    return \"success\";\n  }\n  return statusFromEventType(event.type);\n}\n```\n\nWait but `event.type.split(\".\")[0]` for \"context.started\" → \"context\"; terminal check: context.completed ✓. tool.started → tool.completed ✓ / tool.failed ✓. planner.started → planner.completed ✓. confirmation.required → confirmation.resolved ✓ (family confirmation). action.committed — not inProgress, derives success directly ✓. run.started → family run: run.completed/run.failed exists → success ✓ (start of run shown ✓ after run ends — nice).\n\nBut careful: CONTEXT_PROGRESS events (memory_retrieved etc.) — inProgress? They're progress markers, no terminal counterpart of their own type. They derive \"running\" → perpetual spinner in finished run! THE SCREENSHOT: 读取记忆/召回语义知识/上下文已组装 show empty circles currently (derived undefined? no—). Wait, current behavior: EventDetails uses event.status only (undefined for all harness events) → Circle icon (empty). That's why screenshot shows empty circles, not spinners.\n\nIf I now derive: memory_retrieved → CONTEXT_PROGRESS set → \"running\" → spinner forever in finished run. WORSE than empty circle! So I must handle these too: they're within the context lifecycle; when family has terminal → success.\n\nSo generalize: ANY harness lifecycle event without explicit status, whose family has reached a terminal event, should show... hmm: started/retrying/progress-markers → success when family terminal. What about the family-terminal event itself (context.completed) → derives success already ✓.\n\nSimplest formulation:\n\n```ts\nfunction itemStatus(event: DebugEvent, siblings: DebugEvent[]): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (event.status) return event.status;\n  if (event.type === \"error\") return \"failed\";\n  const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n  if (derived === \"running\") {\n    // 同族生命周期已有终态事件时，started/进度标记按已完成展示，避免结束后仍显示执行中。\n    const family = event.type.split(\".\")[0];\n    const hasTerminal = siblings.some((item) => item.type === `${family}.completed` || item.type === `${family}.failed` || item.type === `${family}.resolved`);\n    if (hasTerminal) return \"success\";\n  }\n  return derived;\n}\n```\n\nClean! Rules:\n- explicit status wins (bridged progress events with status success/running → correct)\n- error → failed\n- derived running (started/retrying/progress markers/confirmation.required) + family terminal exists → success\n- else derived (success for .completed etc., running while live, undefined → empty circle for unknown)\n\nEdge check — live run mid-context: family context has no completed yet → memory_retrieved stays spinner ✓. After context.completed: all context items → success ✓. Live run mid-tool: tool.progress (bridged, explicit status running) → spinner ✓; after tool.completed → progress items → success ✓ (family terminal). Wait — bridged progress events HAVE explicit status (\"success\" from old-graph writer) → `if (event.status) return event.status` → success already, no derivation needed ✓. The tool.progress events WITHOUT explicit status? writer.custom payload always includes status from old-graph writer? The old graph writer always sets status in progress events (looking at nodes: writer({\"type\": \"progress\", \"step\", \"node\", \"status\": \"running\"}) and success/failed variants). So bridged have explicit status ✓. The derivation path mainly serves context/planner/tool-lifecycle/run/confirmation events ✓.\n\nHmm — one more: `tool.started` during live round 2 while round 1's tool.completed exists in siblings: family tool already has terminal from round 1 → round 2's tool.started immediately shows success?! WRONG — should spin while executing!\n\nDamn. Family-level terminal check breaks multi-round: any earlier round's terminal poisons later started markers.\n\nRefine: terminal must come AFTER the started event in sequence (the started event is concluded only by a LATER terminal in the same family):\n\n```ts\nconst hasTerminal = siblings.some((item) => item.sequence > event.sequence && (item.type === `${family}.completed` || ...));\n```\n\nsiblings sorted by sequence (groups preserve order; AllEvents compacted order ✓). sequence: appendExecutionEvent sets incremental sequence ✓. So:\n\n```ts\nfunction itemStatus(event: DebugEvent, siblings: DebugEvent[]): RunStatus | undefined {\n  if (event.status) return event.status;\n  if (event.type === \"error\") return \"failed\";\n  const derived = statusFromEventType(event.type);\n  if (derived === \"running\") {\n    // 同族生命周期在其之后出现终态事件时，started/进度标记按已完成展示，避免运行结束后仍显示执行中。\n    const family = event.type.split(\".\")[0];\n    const concluded = siblings.some(\n      (item) => item.sequence > event.sequence\n        && (item.type === `${family}.completed` || item.type === `${family}.failed` || item.type === `${family}.resolved`),\n    );\n    if (concluded) return \"success\";\n  }\n  return derived;\n}\n```\n\nSequence > — for historical loaded events sequence assigned incrementally in load order ✓. taskOverview synthetic has sequence -1 — but itemStatus isn't applied to it specially (it has explicit status ✓).\n\nMulti-round check: round2 tool.started (seq 50), round1 tool.completed (seq 30) → 30 > 50 false → no conclusion → spinner while live ✓. Round2 tool.completed (seq 60) → 60 > 50 ✓ → round2 started → success ✓. Round1's started (seq 10): round1 completed seq 30 > 10 → success ✓. PERFECT.\n\ncontext items: all in same round; started seq 100, completed seq 140 → all context items before 140 → success ✓.\n\nNow integrate: EventDetails needs siblings. Signature: `<EventDetails event title siblings?>`. Callers:\n1. displayEvents → returns DisplayEvent {key,label,event} — extend to carry siblings? displayEvents receives the group's events — it can precompute and return status... Better: DisplayEvent gains nothing; instead EventDetails takes optional `siblings: DebugEvent[]`. Call sites:\n   - StepCard drawer: `node.events.map((item) => <EventDetails ... />)` — item.event; siblings = the node group's events = displayEvents(nodeEvents) items' events... node.events are DisplayEvents of displayEvents(nodeEvents). siblings = node.events.map(i => i.event)? That allocates... or simpler: itemStatus computed in displayEvents and stored on DisplayEvent as `status`? Then EventDetails uses `display.status ?? event.status ?? ...`.\n\nCleaner: precompute in displayEvents:\n\n```ts\nfunction displayEvents(events: DebugEvent[]): DisplayEvent[] {\n  ...\n  visible.push({ key, label, event, status: itemStatus(event, events) });\n}\n```\n\nDisplayEvent: add `status: RunStatus | undefined`. EventDetails: `<StatusIcon status={display.status ?? ...}`. But EventDetails also renders standalone with just event (AllEvents, StepCard group.events path, TaskBody). Those paths lack siblings...\n\nWhere is EventDetails used?\na. StepCard: node.events.map(item => <EventDetails event={item.event} title={item.label} />) — node.events from displayEvents ✓ can pass item.status.\nb. StepCard: group.events.map(event => <EventDetails event={event} />) — group.events = currentEvents(stepEvents) — has sibling context (pass group.events? currentEvents filtered — sequence comparisons still valid).\nc. TaskBody: node.events (from nodeGroups → displayEvents) and step.display (displayEvents) ✓.\nd. AllEvents: compactedEvents list — siblings = whole compacted array.\n\nOptions: make EventDetails accept `status?: RunStatus` prop computed by caller, or accept siblings. Simplest uniform: EventDetails computes `event.status || statusFromEventType` inline AND accepts optional siblings for the conclusion refinement:\n\nActually simplest implementation with minimal churn: change DisplayEvent to carry resolved status; EventDetails gains optional `status` prop:\n\n```tsx\nfunction EventDetails({ event, title, status }: { event: DebugEvent; title?: string; status?: RunStatus }) {\n  ...<StatusIcon status={status ?? event.status ?? (event.type === \"error\" ? \"failed\" : undefined)} />\n```\n\nCall sites:\na. `<EventDetails event={item.event} title={item.label} status={item.status} />` (node events from displayEvents)\nb. StepCard group.events: `<EventDetails event={event} status={itemStatus(event, group.events)} />`\nc. TaskBody: node.events/step.display — same as (a).\nd. AllEvents: `<EventDetails key event status={itemStatus(event, events)} />`\n\nAnd displayEvents adds status: itemStatus(event, events).\n\nWait — TaskBody/TaskCard node rendering: TaskBody line 650: `node.events.map((item) => <EventDetails key={item.key} event={item.event} title={item.label} />)` and `step.display.map((item) => <EventDetails ...>)`. Add status={item.status}.\n\nAlso — the user asked \"给我整体看一下\" — meaning \"check this overall/throughout\". They want ALL items to show proper status. Let me also check: in the FIRST screenshot earlier today (planning_agent drawer), items showed empty circles — same fix covers.\n\nAlso the group-level icons (node drawer header \"success\") already work via statusForEvents ✓.\n\nHmm — also `displayLabel` uses event.status for fallback labels (\"X结果\"/\"X失败信息\") — harness items have no status → fall through to type-based labels ✓ fine.\n\nOne more consideration — should item status ALSO show \"failed\" when family concluded with .failed? E.g. planner.failed exists; planner.started → success (it started). The planner.failed item itself: type planner.failed — statusFromEventType: doesn't 