 statement. `context.started` / `context.completed` — the type already encodes lifecycle position. Adding a `status` field duplicates information (redundant encoding). REST/SSE event design often keeps types canonical and lets consumers derive.\n- BUT: my `itemStatus` \"family conclusion\" look-ahead (started → success if later completed exists in same family) IS derived/complex logic on the frontend. If the backend emitted a status per event, the frontend wouldn't need it.\n\nHmm wait — actually, let me reconsider the family-conclusion logic. Is it actually needed if... the semantics: \"开始构建上下文\" — what status should this ITEM have in a finished run? The event itself is a transition marker, not a state. The frontend derivation exists because the marker has no intrinsic status.\n\nAlternative cleaner design: backend could emit status on lifecycle events:\n- context.started → status=\"running\"? No wait — that's wrong too: the started event marks the BEGIN; when context.completed arrives later, the started event is historical. The \"correct\" display status of the started marker depends on what happened AFTER it — that's inherently a render-time question, not an event-time question. Even if backend set status=\"running\" on context.started at emit time, the frontend would still need the conclusion logic to flip it to success later.\n\nSo actually — explicit backend status does NOT eliminate the frontend conclusion logic. The event type is the right canonical encoding, and render-time derivation is the correct place. The backend emitting \"status\" per event would be redundant/duplicated state.\n\nBUT — wait. Is that entirely true? Let me think about what \"后端的全部状态就正确\" means. The user might be asking: does the backend correctly emit the full lifecycle (are there missing events/statuses)? E.g.:\n1. Does every started have a completed? context.started/context.completed ✓ (both emitted in _build_context). planner.started/completed — need to check planner emits. tool.started/completed ✓ runtime. finalization.* — check. run.started/completed ✓.\n2. Are there failure events for each phase? planner.failed ✓, tool.failed ✓, context failed?? — if _build_context throws, is a context.failed emitted? Looking at controller._build_context — no try/except emitting context.failed! The context build failure → PlannerFailure?/context_retry path — emits what? From the summary: context temporary failure → context_retry_count += 1, 写受控 RunError. Is there a \"context.failed\" event? statusFromEventType handles .failed suffix generically. If backend never emits context.failed, the context group in a failed-context run would show... the run fails via RunError event? There's an \"error\" event type handled.\n\nLet me actually verify what the backend emits for failure paths. If some failure paths don't emit phase-failed events, the frontend can't show failed status for that module — THAT would be a real backend gap worth fixing.\n\nAlso the run.result event (emitted by _sse_response) — has payload loop_result with status.\n\nSo to answer the user properly I should audit:\n1. Which events the backend emits per phase (success path + failure path)\n2. Whether any phase lacks terminal events (started without completed/failed)\n3. Whether explicit status field on events would be better or redundant\n\nLet me check the controller's emit inventory:\n- run.started, run.completed(?), run.failed, run.timeout, run.cancelled\n- context.started, context.memory_retrieved, context.knowledge_retrieved, context.context_compiled, context.completed — context.failed? grep\n- planner.started, planner.completed, planner.retrying, planner.failed\n- action.committed\n- tool.started, tool.progress, tool.completed, tool.failed, tool.retrying (runtime)\n- confirmation.required, confirmation.resolved\n- observation.recorded\n- finalization.* — what types?\n- run.result, stream.failed (api layer)\n\nLet me grep the controller for all _emit event types and check failure paths (context failure, planner failure, tool failure paths). Also check what status/payload they carry.\n\nThen decide: \n- If context failure path doesn't emit context.failed → add it (backend fix, aligned with \"不兼容\" principle).\n- Otherwise explain why type-convention + render-time derivation is the design (not compatibility), and backend stays unchanged.\n\nActually — wait. Let me also reconsider: is the frontend family-conclusion logic \"看起来就很不靠谱的判断逻辑\"? It relies on the invariant: every X.started is later followed by X.completed/X.failed within the same family. If the backend guarantees this invariant (lifecycle pairing), the logic is sound. If the backend DOESN'T guarantee it (e.g., context build fails silently without context.failed and without context.completed), then started stays spinner forever → the interrupted/partial display. Hmm — but in that case the run itself fails/cancels → run.cancelled/timeout blanket handles the group. Item stays spinner — shows \"interrupted\" state — arguably correct for a crashed phase!\n\nSo the audit: verify the backend emits terminal events for every started in all paths (success, failure, cancel, timeout). Let me grep.","startTime":1791428023744,"lastModifiedTime":1791428023744},{"id":"47b1bd7a-0fe0-4491-8fe6-5096c4aa0a68","type":"TEXT","end":true,"content":"问得对，我先去把后端事件的“生命周期配对”完整盘一遍——如果哪条路径只有 started 没有终态事件，那才是真问题："},{"id":"call_84a41354df79476ab0157845","type":"TOOL","toolName":"run_command","params":{"command":"grep -n 'self\\._emit(\\|writer\\.emit(' /Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis/app/agent/loop_controller/controller.py | grep -o '\"[a-z_.]*\"' | sort | uniq -c","description":"Inventory of event types emitted by controller"},"toolState":"executed","result":{"output":"Command executed successfully with no output.\nExit code: 0","metadata":{"cwd":"/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis","autoRun":true,"state":"completed","runMode":"yolo","taskId":"zt307x","output":"","exitCode":0,"inBackgrou