— 4096 维, 32K\n2. Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B — 4096 维, 32K\n3. Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-4B — 2560 维, 32K\n4. Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B — 1024 维, 32K\n\nThey have 20元 credit on SiliconFlow unused. Question: \"这4个你推荐用哪个，我还有硅基流动的20元没有使用呢\" — which of these 4 do I recommend, considering they have ¥20 SiliconFlow credit.\n\nKey considerations:\n\n1. **Dimension compatibility**: Their Qdrant vector_size = 1024, ES dense_vector 1024 (presumably).\n   - 0.6B: 1024 dims — matches current config exactly!\n   - 4B: 2560 dims (MRL supports custom dims? Qwen3-Embedding series supports MRL custom dimensions 32-2560 per official docs... Actually Qwen3-Embedding models support user-defined output dims 32–2560 for 0.6B/4B/8B via MRL. SiliconFlow API: does it expose dimensions param? SiliconFlow embeddings API does support `dimensions` parameter for Qwen3-Embedding? Not sure — need care. The card shows fixed dims 4096/2560/1024.)\n   - If SiliconFlow doesn't support custom dims, 0.6B @1024 is the only zero-restructure option. 4B@2560/8B@4096 would need Qdrant collection rebuild (vector_size change) + ES mapping change + re-embed — doable but more work.\n\n2. **API compatibility with their client**: They use `OpenAIEmbeddings` with base_url. SiliconFlow is OpenAI-compatible (`https://api.siliconflow.cn/v1`). So provider dashscope path works with base_url swap + api_key. Their config has provider: dashscope — base_url configurable, so pointing to SiliconFlow works mechanically (provider name stays \"dashscope\" branch — it's just an OpenAI-compatible branch; naming is cosmetic).\n\n3. **Quality**: Qwen3-Embedding MTEB scores: 8B (70.58) > 4B (69.44) > 0.6B (64.33) roughly. All are strong; 0.6B is the weakest but still decent (comparable to text-embedding-v3?). For their use case (short metadata recall: table/column descriptions, dimension values in Chinese), 0.6B is adequate; 4B/8B give better recall on harder semantic matches.\n\n4. **The ¥20 credit**: embedding on SiliconFlow is cheap (Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B ~¥0.5/M tokens? Actually SiliconFlow pricing: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B ≈ ¥0.5/M tokens, 4B ≈ ¥2/M?, 8B ≈ ¥4/M? I don't remember exactly. Their usage is tens of millions? No — hundreds of thousands tokens/year. ¥20 would last years regardless.)\n\n5. **Vendor consideration**: Currently they're on Alibaba Bailian (dashscope). Switching to SiliconFlow = new vendor, new API key, different rate limits, but also independent free quota. Their ¥20 on SiliconFlow would last ~forever for embedding at their scale. But vendor fragmentation... Also they still have Bailian free quota (1M) on qwen3.7-text-embedding.\n\n6. **Latency/availability**: SiliconFlow domestic, fine.\n\n7. **Multi-lingual + instruction-aware**: Qwen3-Embedding supports instruction-tuned queries; optional.\n\nMy recommendation: **Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B** — reasons:\n- 1024 dims exactly matches existing Qdrant/ES config → switch = change base_url + model + api_key, then re-embed once. No structural rebuild.\n- Quality ≥ their current text-embedding-v3 for Chinese retrieval (0.6B MTEB 64.3 vs v3 ~? v3 is decent; qwen3-0.6B is newer generation; likely comparable or better; and they can judge via recall).\n- ¥20 lasts years at their volume.\n\nBut wait — is 0.6B actually better than their current text-embedding-v3? text-embedding-v3 MTEB Chinese ~? v3 is solid. Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B MTEB multilingual 64.33; text-embedding-v4 scores ~70+? Hmm. Bailian's text-embedding-v4 (which they could use, 0.0007元/K = 0.7元/M) is likely stronger than qwen3-0.6B. And qwen3.7-text-embedding (their free 1M) is stronger still.\n\nSo the honest ranking for QUALITY: qwen3.7-text-embedding (Bailian, free 1M then 0.5/M) > Qwen3-Embedding-8B/4B (SiliconFlow) > text-embedding-v4 > v3 ≈ Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B?\n\nHmm — actually I should be careful with claims I can't verify. Let me think about what's defensible:\n- Qwen3-Embedding-8B: MTEB multilingual #1-tier (70.58)\n- 4B: 69.44\n- 0.6B: 64.33\n- text-embedding-v3: older gen; Chinese decent but not at qwen3 level\n- qwen3.7-text-embedding: claims +20% over v4\n\nDimension constraint:\n- If they want to stay at 1024 without touching infra: 0.6B (SiliconFlow) or qwen3.7-text-embedding with dimensions=1024 (Bailian supports custom dims 256-2560 ✓).\n- If they're willing to rebuild collections with new vector_size: 4B@2560 or 8B@4096 on SiliconFlow, or qwen3.7@2560 on Bailian.\n\nTheir ¥20 SiliconFlow credit: at 0.6B pricing (~¥0.5/M), ¥20 = 40M tokens = years of their usage. 8B pricing maybe ¥4/M? Hmm — SiliconFlow Qwen3-Embedding-8B might be ~¥1.5-2/M... not sure. Even so, their annual usage <5M tokens → ¥20 covers years even at 8B.\n\nSo the credit size is NOT the deciding factor — ANY of the 4 fits within ¥20 for years. The deciding factors:\n1. Quality (8B > 4B > 0.6B)\n2. Infra rebuild scope (1024 = no rebuild; 2560/4096 = rebuild collections + ES mapping)\n3. Stability/vendor\n\nBest engineering answer: \n- **推荐 Qwen3-Embedding-8B if willing to rebuild once to 4096 dims** — best quality, ¥20 covers years, and since they're ALREADY re-embedding for the model switch, bumping dimensions costs nothing extra (collections must be recreated anyway when switching models... wait — no! If dimension changes, collection must be deleted and recreated with new vector_size; if dimension same, they could in principle keep collections and just overwrite vectors. Since a model switch requires re-embedding ALL vectors anyway, the marginal cost of ALSO changing vector_size is just \"delete + recreate collection with new size\" — which the rebuild flow probably does anyway (delete/recreate collections). Hmm — their rebuild flow: does it delete-recreate collections or upsert into existing? If upsert requires matching vector_size, switching models with same dim = no infra change; different dim = one extra step (recreate collections with new vector_size in config). Small.\n\nSo honestly: since re-embedding is required regardless, the dimension change adds only trivial work (update qdrant.vector_size + ES mapping dims + recreate collections). Then the quality argument dominates → recommend 8B? But 8B embedding latency at query time: each query embeds ~50 tokens — 8B model inference is slower (maybe 100-300ms vs 30-80ms for 0.6B) — still fine for their pipeline (context build takes seconds).\n\nBut wait — one more consideration: 32K context all same. Multilingual fine.\n\nHmm, also instruction-aware: Qwen3-Embedding benefits from instruction prefix on queries; optional.\n\nAlso SiliconFlow vs Bailian vendor: they ALSO have qwen3.7-text-embedding free 1M on Bailian which is likely comparable to 8B quality (claims +20% over v4; v4 ≈ qwen3-embedding level?). Honestly qwen3.7-text-embedding on Bailian is probably the best quality-per-effort: keep current vendor, custom dims, free 1M then cheap.\n\nBut the user explicitly asks \"这4个你推荐用哪个，我还有硅基流动的20元\" — they want to use the SiliconFlow credit. I should answer their question directly (which of the 4), while noting the alternative.\n\nRecommendation: **Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B if you want zero infra change**, or **Qwen3-Embedding-8B if you want max quality and accept recreating collections at 4096 dims**. Given they're already doing a full re-embed, I'd lean 8B... but hold on — latency and cost at scale: 8B for query-time embedding on every message; SiliconFlow 8B embedding price — let me recall actual SiliconFlow pricing (as of 2025): BAAI/bge-m3 free; Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B ¥0.5/M? Actually I believe SiliconFlow pricing: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B = ¥0.5/M tokens, 4B = ¥2/M, 8B = ¥4/M (roughly). Their annual usage maybe 2-5M tokens → 8B = ¥8-20/year. The ¥20 covers ~1-3 years. OK.\n\nBut honestly — for THEIR use case (short Chinese metadata/dimension-value recall, not hard NLP tasks), 0.6B vs 8B recall difference on their data is likely small. The 0.6B keeps infra untouched and latency lowest. The pragmatic engineering choice: 0.6B. The maximalist choice: 8B.\n\nHmm, I need to give ONE recommendation. Decision framework:\n- Their pipeline: recall quality directly drives answer quality (wrong table/column recalled = wrong SQL). Metadata descriptions are short but the matching problem (natural language question → column descriptions) is exactly where stronger models help. 0.6B is the weakest of the series (MTEB 64.3) — noticeably below 4B/8B (~69-70.6). That gap is real for recall.\n- They're re-embedding everything anyway → dimension bump is nearly free.\n- ¥20 covers years even at 8B.\n- Latency: query embed on 8B — SiliconFlow embedding latency for 8B maybe ~200-500ms — acceptable in a pipeline that runs seconds.\n\nSo my recommendation: **Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B** for recall quality, accepting the one-time vector_size 1024→4096 change (config + recreate collections + ES mapping). Runner-up 0.6B if they want zero structural change / lowest latency. Also note: 4B@2560 is the middle path and MRL dims... does SiliconFlow support custom dimensions param for these? I believe SiliconFlow's /v1/embeddings supports `dimensions` for Qwen3-Embedding (they advertise MRL support). If it does, they could run 8B at 1024 dims with zero infra change and most of the quality! That would be the ideal: 8B @ 1024 custom dims. But I'm not certain SiliconFlow exposes it. I could check the docs quickly via web fetch. Let me do a quick search to verify SiliconFlow dimensions support + pricing. That's worth verifying before recommending.\n\nAlso verify: qwen3.7-text-embedding-flash free (1M until 11/30) as Bailian alternative.\n\nActually — keep the scope tight. I'll do one quick search on SiliconFlow Qwen3-Embedding pricing/dimensions support, then answer.\n\nNote: their embedding client passes `dimensions=self.config.dimensions` to OpenAIEmbeddings → sends `dimensions` param in request. If SiliconFlow ignores/errors on it... For 0.6B default is 1024 so even if param ignored/error, set dimensions accordingly. If SiliconFlow supports dimensions for 8B, set 1024 → zero infra change + top quality.\n\nLet me search.","startTime":1791428023749,"lastModifiedTime":1791428023749},{"id":"507f42a8-ea4c-4842-a046-2aa4c51253d3","type":"TEXT","end":true,"content":"这 4 个的选择关键在两点：维度兼容性和是否支持自定义维度。我先查一下硅基流动的 Qwen3-Embedding 是否支持自定义输出维度（决定要不要动 Qdrant/ES 结构）："},{"id":"call_01da3697b990463caaebbfc3","type":"TOOL","toolName":"web_search","params":{"max_results":"6","query":"硅基流动 SiliconFlow Qwen3-Embedding-8B dimensions 参数 自定义维度 价格 2026"},"toolState":"executed","result":{"output":{"$blob":"sha256:2fad1167dddbe005ea77202066808237d7c2faffe5dba8591096c41eb26e677f","version":1,"encoding":"utf8","byteLength":8249,"compression":"none"},"metadata":{"sites":[{"content":"| Qwen3-Embedding-8B | 8B | 70.58 | 61.69 | 80.89 | 74.00 | 57.65 | 10.06 | 28.66 | 86.40 | 65.63 | 70.88 | 81.08 | [...] | Qwen3-Embedding-4B | 4B | 72.27 | 73.51 | 75.46 | 77.89 | 83.34 | 66.05 | 77.03 | 61.26 |\n| Qwen3-Embedding-8B | 8B | 73.84 | 75.00 | 76.97 | 80.08 | 84.23 | 66.99 | 78.21 | 63.53 | [...] | gemini-embedding-exp-03-07  73.3 | 67.67 | 90.05 | 59.39 | 87.7 | 48.59 | 64.35 | 85.29 | 38.28 |\n| Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B | 0.6B | 70.70 | 64.88 | 85.76 | 54.05 | 84.37 | 48.18 | 61.83 | ...","title":"Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B · Hugging Face","url":"https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B","favicon":null,"source":null},{"content":"###### 模型\n\n###### 产品\n\n###### 定价\n\n###### 文档\n\n###### 博客\n\n###### 关于\n\n###### 联系\n\n开始\n\n🎉 Kimi-K3 可以在 SiliconFlow 上使用。现在就尝试吧。\n\n🎉 Kimi-K3 可以在 SiliconFlow 上使用。现在就尝试吧。\n\n模型\n\nQwen3-Embedding-8B\n\n# Qwen3-Embedding-8B\n\n## Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-8B\n\n打开模型广场\n\nAPI 参考\n\n关于Qwen3-Embedding-8B\n\nQwen3-Embedding-8B 是 Qwen3 Embedding 系列中的最新专有模型，专为文本嵌入和排名任务设计。该模型建立在 Qwen3 系列的密集基础模型之上，拥有 8B 参数，支持最长 32K 的上下文长度，可以生成最多 4096 维度的嵌入。该模型继承了出色的多语言功能，支持超过 100 种语言，并具备长文本理解和推理能力。截至 2025 年 6 月 5 日，它在 MTEB 多语言排行榜上排名第一（分数为 70.58），并展示了在各...","title":"Qwen3-Embedding-8B - 模型信息、参数、基准 - SiliconFlow\"","url":"https://www.siliconflow.com/zh/models/qwen3-embedding-8