:"ac355806-1506-444f-b11d-dd846840b439","type":"REASON","end":true,"content":"`_trace_plan` strips the raw query/reason and produces an auditable compact reason: `working=True;memory_types=semantic,episodic;rag=False`. That's exactly what the controller can emit as `context.plan` — structured fields + the compact reason. \n\nNow implement the backend `context.plan` event. In `controller._build_context`, after `context.context_compiled` (or before it), emit:\n\n```python\n        plan = compiled_context.trace.retrieval_plan\n        self._emit(\n            run_ref,\n            \"context.plan\",\n            phase=phase,\n            iteration=int(state[\"harness\"][\"iteration\"]),\n            payload={\n                \"include_working\": plan.include_working,\n                \"memory_types\": [item.value for item in plan.memory_types],\n                \"use_rag\": plan.use_rag,\n                \"reason\": plan.reason,\n                \"candidate_count\": compiled_context.trace.candidate_count,\n                \"selected_count\": compiled_context.trace.selected_count,\n                \"token_count\": compiled_context.token_count,\n            },\n        )\n```\n\nWhere exactly? Between context.knowledge_retrieved and context.context_compiled makes lifecycle sense: started → memory_retrieved → knowledge_retrieved → plan (决策) → context_compiled (结果) → completed. Actually plan happens logically FIRST (decides what to recall) but we only have the compiled result here — order in stream: emit it after knowledge_retrieved, before context_compiled. Fine.\n\nFrontend A档: in deriveProgressMilestones, the \"understand\" milestone — currently:\n```ts\n    milestones.push({\n      key: \"understand\",\n      label: \"理解问题并召回上下文\",\n      status: boundary?.type === \"context.failed\" ? \"failed\" : boundary ? \"success\" : \"running\",\n      durationMs: boundary ? eventTimestampMs(boundary) - contextStart : undefined,\n    });\n```\nAdd detail: route decision + recall