 we call `useMessage()` and `useAuiState(...)`. We didn't add hooks... wait, did we? Our changes to chat-session-view.tsx: removed avatar, changed JSX, added MarkdownContent import, restyled button. No new hooks.\n\nBut hold on — `getSnapshot should be cached` — likely from @assistant-ui/react internals (useAuiState/useMessage use useSyncExternalStore). Did our clean `node_modules` rebuild change assistant-ui version? package.json says `@assistant-ui/react ^0.14.16` — with no package-lock.json previously, the reinstall may have bumped to a newer 0.14.x or different transitive deps, potentially introducing this bug. Hmm, but the error appears specifically now after our changes... \n\nWait — actually, the more likely culprit: **our MarkdownContent renders during streaming with `displayText`** — no, that's not store related.\n\nLet me think again about \"The result of getSnapshot should be cached\". React's useSyncExternalStore warning-as-error: happens when getSnapshot returns a different value on every call (e.g., new object/array each time). In assistant-ui, useAuiState((state) => ...) — selectors returning new references each render (like `.at(-1)?.id` — that's a primitive string, fine; `state.thread.isRunning` boolean, fine).\n\nWhere could a non-cached snapshot come from? `useMessage()` — returns message object; if internally getSnapshot builds a new object per call... that's assistant-ui's bug, version-dependent.\n\nHmm wait — but maybe the loop is caused by OUR component: `conversationMeta(message)` or something building new objects? Those existed before. The page worked before our change (user was chatting). The regression came with commit 57f73f2 or the node_modules rebuild.\n\nKey insight: node_modules was rebuilt WITHOUT a lockfile — so ALL packages floated to latest matching semver. `@assistant-ui/react` ^0.14.16 could have jumped from 0.14.16 to 0.14.20+ with a new getSnapshot caching bug, or radix/react versions floated. The first screenshot's error is at SessionPage render — an infinite render loop in the whole view.\n\nDiagnosis path:\n1. Check installed @assistant-ui/react version now vs before (git shows package.json unchanged for it — but installed version could differ).\n2. Check if MarkdownContent could cause an infinite loop: react-markdown is pure — no.\n3. Actually wait — there's another candidate: our `MarkdownContent text={displayText + (showStreamingCursor ? \" ▍\" : \"\")}` — displayText from useMessage... no loop.\n\nHmm, but \"什么也不展示\" + \"输入内容后跳到历史记录\" — the user says after typing input, nothing displays and it navigates to history? \"新建页面输入内容调整到历史记录\" — probably \"跳转到历史记录\" — meaning the conversation gets created/redirected to history page? That's app behavior on send (router push to /sessions/[id]?). Hard to know. The console error is the concrete signal: infinite loop.\n\nThe React error \"The result of getSnapshot should be cached to avoid an infinite loop\" is thrown by useSyncExternalStore when getSnapshot is impure. Let me check @assistant-ui/react's useAuiState/store implementation in the installed version. Also compare installed version vs 0.14.16.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider: maybe it IS our bug, introduced in AssistantMessageBubble. Look at what changed in that component... `const message = useMessage();` — was already there. `useAuiState` — already there.\n\nBUT: there's one new thing — we now render `<MarkdownContent>` during streaming for EVERY message (before, streaming showed plain text). react-markdown re-parses per render — heavy but not a loop.\n\nInfinite loop candidates in React: a component that setState during render, or useSyncExternalStore with uncached getSnapshot. The error explicitly names getSnapshot caching → useSyncExternalStore. Which store? assistant-ui's (useAuiState) or zustand? @assistant-ui/core/store use zustand v5. zustand v5 uses useSyncExternalStore with getSnapshot [store.getState()] — cached. Unless a selector in useAuiState returns fresh object...\n\nOur selectors: `state.thread.isRunning` (bool), `state.thread.messages.at(-1)?.id` (string | undefined) — primitives. useMessage() — hook from assistant-ui, internal.\n\nHmm — but these were all present BEFORE and worked. What changed in the component tree: MarkdownContent (new), JSX restructure. Could JSX restructure break assistant-ui's MessagePrimitive.Root context? No.\n\nSo prime suspect: **dependency float during clean reinstall**. Check installed version of @assistant-ui/react and friends. Let me compare: `cat node_modules/@assistant-ui/react/package.json | grep version` and look at what useAuiState does in installed build; check zustand version; check react version exact.\n\nAlso possible: npm installed a NEW @assistant-ui/react 0.15/0.14.x with breaking change + bug. Let me check.\n\nPlan:\n1. `grep '\"version\"' node_modules/@assistant-ui/react/package.json`\n2. Check zustand/react versions.\n3. Look at the assistant-ui source for getSnapshot in useAuiState (node_modules/@assistant-ui/react/dist or @assistant-ui/store).\n4. Possibly pin to previously working version. Do we know the previously installed version? No lockfile existed, so unknown. But git history doesn't record it. We could pin \"^0.14.16\" → exact 0.14.16? But maybe 0.14.16 also has the bug and previous install was OLDER? ^0.14.16 can't install older than 0.14.16. Previous installs used ^0.14.16 too → previous version ≥0.14.16. If current is newer 0.14.x, pinning exact previous patch (e.g. 0.14.16) might restore.\n\nWait — actually, is it possible the bug is genuinely OURS and I'm missing it? Let me re-read my AssistantMessageBubble edit... \n\n`<MarkdownContent text={displayText + (showStreamingCursor ? \" ▍\" : \"\")} />` — during streaming, every SSE chunk → parent re-renders → fine.\n\nHmm, one more: in `AssistantChatRuntime`, `convertMessage: (message) => message as ThreadMessage` — existed.\n\nAnd \"输入内容后...跳转到历史记录\" — on send, the app appends user message via onNew → probably navigates via router to /sessions/[id]. The crash then happens on the session page render.\n\nHonestly the strongest hypothesis remains dep float. But let me verify empirically: read the installed @assistant-ui/react version + inspect useAuiState implementation for getSnapshot caching.\n\nActually — hold on. One more candidate I should seriously consider: **Zustand v5 + our useAuiState selector**. `useAuiState((state) => state.thread.messages.at(-1)?.id)` — `.at(-1)` on array — returns string — cached fine.\n\nBut `useMessage()` — in assistant-ui 0.14, useMessage gets message from context... it's `useSyncExternalStore`-based? If it constructs `{...message}` per call → bug. Version-specific.\n\nEmpirical checks:\n1. Installed versions: @assistant-ui/react, @assistant-ui/store, @assistant-ui/core, zustand, react.\n2. Grep the dist for \"getSnapshot\" near useAuiState to see if it returns fresh objects.\n\nThen fix: pin versions to restore (add package-lock now exists — we could `npm ci`? lockfile was JUST generated with the floated versions, so npm ci reproduces the broken state). Better: `npm install @assistant-ui/react@0.14.16 --save-exact`? But was 0.14.16 the working version? Unknown — maybe. Alternatively pin zustand (v5.0.14 vs previous?). \n\nHmm wait, actually — I realize I should check ONE more of our changes first, because there's something I overlooked: **the streaming cursor string `\" ▍\"` concatenated INSIDE markdown** — no.\n\nAnd... `conversationMeta(message)`? Existed.\n\nOK — also consider: the error might predate our change but only triggers now because... no, user says it's a fresh bug after our change (\"有bug输入内容后，什么也不展示\").\n\nEmpirical route. Also quick test: `git stash` the frontend changes and see if error persists? The changes are committed (57f73f2). Revert test = checkout previous commit for those files — but node_modules float is independent of files. Quick isolation: `git stash` won't help since committed. Could `git revert --no-commit 57f73f2` locally to test, restart dev, see if error persists → distinguishes code vs deps. That's a solid binary test. But restarting dev server is on user's terminal (npm run dev is running in their environment with Turbopack — from screenshot \"Next.js 16.3.5 Turbopack\"). I can't easily restart their dev server... Actually I can run my own build/typecheck but runtime behavior needs their browser. Hmm — I can start my own `npm run dev` on another port and curl the page? The infinite loop happens client-side (hydration) — curl gives SSR HTML only; the loop may manifest during SSR too (React SSR useSyncExternalStore getSnapshot warning → 500?). The screenshot shows the error overlay in browser — client-side.\n\nBetter empirical approach: inspect the installed assistant-ui code for the bug. If found, pin the version to a known-good one. Let me look at versions & code.\n\nLet me check:\n- installed version of @assistant-ui/react (and what `useAuiState` looks like)\n- installed zustand version\n- react exact version\n\nAlso — Next.js floated too? package.json next ^16.2.6 — screenshot shows \"Next.js 16.3.5\"! Before, was it 16.2.x? The floating install bumped next to 16.3.5. Next 16.3 could itself have the getSnapshot caching issue with useSyncExternalStore during hydration? The error is thrown by React itself. React version: ^19.2.6 → may have floated to 19.2.x/19.3? \n\nThe \"result of getSnapshot should be cached\" error in React 19.2+: React checks `getSnapshot()` stability during render and THROWS \"The result of getSnapshot should be cached to avoid an infinite loop\" when it changes — this is React's own invariant. It triggers when a store's getSnapshot returns new reference per call. Who? zustand v5's `useStore` uses `useSyncExternalStoreWithSelector`? No — zustand v5 dropped the shim, uses React.useSyncExternalStore(api.subscribe, () => api.getState(), ...) — getState returns cached state. Selectors applied after — with useSyncExternalStore, if selector returns new object → infinite loop detected as \"getSnapshot should be cached\"? Actually the getSnapshot check happens on the getSnapshot passed to useSyncExternalStore, before selector... React calls getSnapshot twice; if results differ (Object.is) → warning/error. zustand passes `api.getState` — stable. assistant-ui's own store (auiState) — custom store impl in @assistant-ui/store. If useAuiState(selector) internally uses useSyncExternalStore with a getSnapshot that runs the selector → selector returning fresh object → THIS error. Our selectors return primitives though...\n\nUNLESS — useMessage() internally: `useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, () => ({ message: ..., ... }))` — fresh object per call → would ALWAYS crash, not just now. So version float changed something. Let me just look at the installed code.\n\nActually, WAIT. Let me reconsider one of our changes again — `useAuiState((state) => state.thread.messages.at(-1)?.id)` — unchanged. `threadIsRunning` unchanged...\n\nHmm, but actually — is it possible the crash is in the SIDEBAR (the \"跳转到历史记录\" mentions history list) — sidebar.tsx has a KNOWN pre-existing tsc error: `Type 'Record<string, unknown>[]' is not assignable to type 'SidebarConversation[]'` — sidebar fetches conversations and maps to plain objects! If sidebar uses useSyncExternalStore or a context with uncached snapshot... The pre-existing tsc error at sidebar.tsx:90 — `SidebarConversation[]` vs `Record<string, unknown>[]`. And the user says \"跳转到历史记录\" (jumps to history) — the crash might be in the history/sidebar component that renders after navigating!\n\nBut the error overlay names SessionPage (13:10) as the component — `return <ChatSessionView conversationId={id} />`. So crash inside ChatSessionView render tree.\n\nOK, enough speculation — gather facts:\n1. Installed versions: @assistant-ui/react, @assistant-ui/core, @assistant-ui/store, zustand, react, next\n2. Grep assistant-ui dist for the getSnapshot pattern\n3. Read ChatSessionView top-level (the c