tore` getSnapshot caching check to throw/re-render-loop in cases 19.2 tolerated. If assistant-ui 0.14.29 has internal selectors that return fresh objects (new in 0.14.17..0.14.29), then 19.3 throws where 19.2 warned... or if 0.14.16's internals had the same object selectors, then 19.2.6 tolerated?? Can't both.\n\nActually — maybe I should check what useAuiState looks like in store 0.2.22 vs 0.2.14. useAuiState is called in OUR component (threadIsRunning, lastMessageId) AND inside Thread/Message primitives. Let me look at the installed store package.\n\nActually — wait. STOP. Let me reconsider the whole thing from a different angle: maybe it's NOT assistant-ui at all. The error overlay pointed at SessionPage/ChatSessionView. What if OUR code has a genuine new infinite render loop introduced by... MarkdownContent? No hooks there besides pure render. AssistantMessageBubble — all hooks pre-existing.\n\nHmm, but what about \"输入内容后什么也不展示\" + the second screenshot showing the EMPTY thread rendering primitives layout mode... Actually the second screenshot with purple/green overlay boxes is the React 19 + Next dev \"component stack\" overlay (Next 16.3's new dev overlay shows component names over the page). The thread is EMPTY there — user hadn't sent yet or messages lost.\n\nHmm wait — actually there's one more thing I changed: I restyled ChatRunSummaryChip and the fallback button — pure className changes. And the message render restructure. None add loops.\n\nBUT — one REAL possibility I overlooked: **`conversationMeta(message)`** and `getMessageText`... pre-existing.\n\nOK here's the thing — I keep speculating. The empirical isolation test: run the app with ONLY the version rollback (no code revert) and see if the crash disappears. That's user-assisted testing. To minimize user round-trips, I'll do the rollback of the most suspicious delta set in one shot: assistant-ui suite + react + react-dom to floor versions. next stays 16.3.5 (if still broken, downgrade next too).\n\nWait — actually, one more cheap code check first: the store package's useAuiState in 0.2.22. Let me look at how useAuiState subscribes. If IT has the object-returning selector issue with thread.messages (e.g. `useAuiState` without selector returns whole state — stable? getState() returns the store object — stable ref). Our calls pass primitive selectors. Fine.\n\nAnd ThreadPrimitive.Viewport internals: `useThreadViewport` etc. Could contain `(s) => s.thread.messages.slice(...)`... Let me grep the INSTALLED react dist for object-returning selector patterns quickly:\n- `.messages.slice(`, `.messages.filter(`, `.messages.map(`, `({ ` near useRuntimeState callers.\n\nThat's hard to grep reliably. Skip — go empirical.\n\nRollback plan:\n```\nnpm install --save-exact @assistant-ui/react@0.14.16 @assistant-ui/core@0.2.12 @assistant-ui/store@0.2.14 @assistant-ui/tap@0.6.1 react@19.2.6 react-dom@19.2.6\n```\nThis also writes package-lock.json (now maintained). Then user refreshes browser (Turbopack will reload). If fixed → commit package.json changes. If not → next@16.2.6.\n\nHmm — but wait, should I pin react to 19.2.6 in the same shot? If I change two variables and it's fixed, I won't know which one mattered — does it matter? For stability, what matters is a known-good set + lockfile. Both pins together restore the \"as-developed\" state. Fine — one shot, both. Actually hmm, react 19.2.6 exact + assistant-ui floors = the declared floors. Good.\n\nBut actually, let me think about whether react@19.2.6 might be problematic to install — react 19.2.6 exists (was installed before per package.json ^19.2.6... the floor was written when 19.2.6 was current). Yes exists.\n\nnext: ^16.2.6 → now 16.3.5. Keep 16.3.5 — Next 16.3 is probably fine; and downgrading next changes dev tooling. If needed later.\n\nzustand: keep 5.0.15.\n\nRun the install. Then tell user to refresh and retest. Also update package.json floors? --save-exact writes exact versions into package.json — good (\"不做无用兼容