match the lockfile for the pinned packages (they'll likely be newer than my npm floors — check whether the drift bug reappears! If pnpm-lock.yaml has e.g. assistant-ui 0.14.25 that worked fine, then the bug was npm-layout/other, or the specific 0.14.29).\n\nHmm wait — but if the lockfile had 0.14.29 already (if lockfile was updated recently), then... Let me just look.\n\n5. Update package.json: should I keep the exact pins? If pnpm-lock.yaml restores newer versions, the exact pins (0.14.16) in package.json will FORCE downgrade during pnpm install and rewrite the lockfile. Decision needed: if lockfile's versions differ from my pins, which wins? The user's intent: use pnpm, restore proper state. Best: revert my exact pins in package.json back to `^` ranges as they were (keep react-markdown + remark-gfm additions), delete package-lock.json, run pnpm install → lockfile governs → original working versions restored. If the infinite-loop bug reappears with those, THEN we know the bug is real in those versions and pin deliberately via pnpm.\n\nWait, but careful: the loop bug WAS observed with 0.14.29+react 19.3 (npm layout). If pnpm-lock has exactly those versions, pnpm install would reproduce the crash. Then we'd pin via package.json exact + pnpm. If lockfile has older versions, crash shouldn't reappear.\n\nLet me check the state first. Also check pnpm availability (`pnpm --version`).\n\nThen also: git — package-lock.json was committed in 6bfb458; now we'd delete it and commit pnpm state. The commit history will show the churn; fine — explain to user.\n\nAlso revert the exact pins? My pins changed package.json from \"^0.14.16\" to \"0.14.16\" etc. If lockfile restores newer, exact pins conflict. Options:\n- Keep pins until user confirms the bug is gone with pnpm+lockfile versions. If lockfile versions == my floor pins anyway, no conflict.\n- Actually cleanest: `git show HEAD~1:frontend/package.json` to get pre-pin package.json, restore the ^ ranges for the six packages I pinned, keep react-markdown/remark-gfm additions. Then pnpm install resolves per lockfile.\n\nHmm — but the bug WAS real under npm. Was it version-related or npm-layout-related? The arborist bug aside, the runtime infinite loop is browser-side — package layout (pnpm vs npm symlink structure) doesn't change runtime behavior... EXCEPT version resolution differs. With pnpm's strict node_modules (no hoisting), imports resolve to exactly the locked versions. With npm's hoisting, duplicates/hoisted versions could mix (e.g., two react copies?? or assistant-ui resolving a different zustand). Actually! That's a real runtime difference: npm hoisting could produce version skew (e.g. @assistant-ui/react seeing a hoisted zustand 5.0.15 vs its own), but the loop... \n\nHonestly: the runtime crash is explained by VERSIONS (0.14.29/19.3.0), regardless of package manager layout. The layout only explains the npm arborist crash. So: if pnpm-lock.yaml pins the OLD versions (0.14.16-era), pnpm install restores them → bug gone. My npm floor pins are then redundant but harmless-ish (they'd conflict if lockfile has newer). \n\nEnough — gather facts: find pnpm-lock.yaml, check pnpm, check lockfile's recorded versions for the key packages. Then decide.","startTime":1791428023750,"lastModifiedTime":1791428023750},{"id":"08b2c55e-55f2-43b3-91e6-d13ed41c0bdb","type":"TEXT","end":true,"content":"这是我该早发现的——之前 npm 装不上报 arborist 错误，其实就是 pnpm 的 symlink 结构被 npm 操作的典型症状（python 工具链 skill 里也写着你的标准是 pnpm）。马上核实 lockfile 状态和里面记录的真实基准版本："},{"id":"call_8e5518dda3094d1bb258d319","type":"TOOL","toolName":"run_command","params":{"command":"cd \"/Users/z523/Desktop/zizhi/ai_Python/ai_data_analysis\" && ls frontend/pnpm-lock.yaml pnpm-lock.yaml 2>/dev/null; printf \"---- pnpm: \"; pnpm --version 2>/dev/null || printf \"not found\\n\"; printf \"---- workspace: \"; ls pnpm-workspace.yaml 2>/dev/null || printf \"none\\n\"","description":"Locate pnpm-lock.yaml and check pnpm availability"},"toolState":"executed","resu