on":45262},"hasUnpairedToolUse":false,"children":[{"id":"7200ae47-d944-412b-bf73-8b5a7c4e2b87","type":"REASON","end":true,"content":"The user asks: what is `SimpleNamespace` here, and what does this block express overall?\n\n```python\nruntime = SimpleNamespace(\n    context=self.context,\n    stream_writer=self.event_writer.custom,\n)\n```\n\nFrom the earlier session work (summarized context): LangGraph passes a `Runtime` object to nodes (`from langgraph.runtime import Runtime`, nodes have signature `(state, runtime: Runtime)` and access `runtime.context` / `runtime.stream_writer`). When the Harness tools call these node functions **directly** (not through graph orchestration), there's no LangGraph Runtime object — so they fabricate a compatible shim: SimpleNamespace with `.context` (the AgentContext) and `.stream_writer` (the event bridge function). The nodes only ever touch those two attributes, so a SimpleNamespace duck-types the Runtime.\n\nWhy SimpleNamespace: it's stdlib `types.SimpleNamespace` — an object whose keyword args become attributes (`runtime.context`, `runtime.stream_writer` accessible with dot notation). Lightweight fake object for duck-typed interfaces.\n\nWhy `stream_writer=self.event_writer.custom`: in-graph, LangGraph's `runtime.stream_writer` is a sync callback the node calls to emit custom events (get_stream_writer / runtime.stream_writer — emits into graph's stream as \"custom\" mode events). In query_data tool path, the graph emits custom events that the tool catches via `stream_mode=\"custom\"` and then bridges with `self.event_writer.custom(payload)`. In the direct-call path (analyze/report), there's no graph stream — so the tool wires `runtime.stream_writer` **directly to** `writer.custom`, i.e., whatever the node \"streams\" goes straight into the Harness event pipeline as tool.progress. This matches writer.custom's docstring: \"把 LangGraph custom 事件归一化为前端稳定的 tool.progress\".\n\nAlso note in the user's quote, line 48 docstring now read