s\"],\n            status=status,\n            phase=phase,\n            terminal_intent=terminal_intent,\n        )\n        return state\n```\n\n\"这个是不是有点多余\" — is this a bit redundant/superfluous?\n\nThe user applies the \"单调用点/多余封装\" lens. Let me analyze:\n\n`_transition` is a thin wrapper around `transition_harness_state`. What does it add?\n1. It mutates `state[\"harness\"]` in place (assigns the new harness dict into the outer state dict) AND returns the state.\n2. Call sites do `state = self._transition(state, status=..., phase=...)` — 10+ call sites in _run and elsewhere.\n\nIs it 多余? Let's think from the user's \"call-site count\" principle — but this is the OPPOSITE case: it has MANY call sites (I saw ~10 in _run alone: BUILD_CONTEXT, PLAN, VALIDATE_ACTION, EXECUTE_TOOL, HANDLE_TOOL_RESULT, RECORD_OBSERVATION, next BUILD_CONTEXT, plus in _finalize, _pause_for_confirmation, etc.). So per the user's own validated principle (单调用点不封装 → 多调用点封装合理), this wrapper is justified.\n\nWhat does the wrapper actually do vs calling transition_harness_state directly?\n\nWithout wrapper, each call site would be:\n```python\nstate[\"harness\"] = transition_harness_state(\n    state[\"harness\"],\n    status=HarnessStatusType.RUNNING,\n    phase=LoopPhaseStatusType.BUILD_CONTEXT,\n)\n```\nWith wrapper: `state = self._transition(state, status=..., phase=...)`.\n\nDifferences:\n1. Less repetition at 10+ call sites — the `state[\"harness\"] = ...(state[\"harness\"], ...)` pattern (passing the sub-dict in and assigning back) is spelled once.\n2. Consistent keyword-only interface with type hints.\n3. The mutation-in-place + return-state pattern: why both? Because call sites use `state = self._transition(state, ...)` — reassignment style. Since dicts are mutable, `state[\"harness\"]` assignment mutates the dict in place, so the return is technically unnecessary... but it matches the functional style used throughout _run (`state = self._transition(...)`, `compiled_context = ...`). Actually — is the return redundant? If the caller did `self._transition(state, ...)` without assignment, it would still work (mutation in place). But all call sites reassign for clarity. Hmm.\n\nIs the return 冗余? It's a style choice: the codebase treats state transitions as producing a new state value (`state = self._transition(...)`) which reads like the immutable-style state threading. The in-place mutation means the return is not *necessary*, but:\n- Removing the return would change call sites to `self._transition(state, ...)` — inconsistent with `state[\"harness\"][\"x\"] = ...` direct mutations elsewhere... actually the codebase mixes both styles already (`state[\"harness\"][\"planner_retry_count\"] = 0` mutates directly).\n- The return + reassignment makes the data flow explicit at each call site: \"state changes here\". This is deliberate readability.\n\nAlso — why does the wrapper exist at all vs calling transition_harness_state directly everywhere? Because transition_harness_state takes and returns ONLY the harness sub-dict, while the controller works with the WHOLE state (HarnessGraphState = user_id/conversation_id/.../harness). The wrapper owns the \"harness is a sub-dict of the outer state\" knowledge. If that knowledge were spread across 10+ call sites, changing the state layout (e.g., harness moving to state[\"control\"]) would touch every call site.\n\nSo the answer structure:\n1. 不多余——正好是\"单调用点不封装\"原则的反面:它有 10+ 个调用点,是高频重复模式。\n2. 它包掉的知识:`state[\"harness\"]` 这个\"子字典进出\"的搬运。没有它,每个调用点都要写 `state[\"harness\"] = transition_harness_state(state[\"harness\"], ...)`——底层知识(状态布局)复制到每个调用点。\n3. 一层薄但不是零:它把\"外层 state / 内层 harness\"的边界钉在 controller 这边;transition_harness_state 只认识 harness 字典,不认识整个 state。分层:controller 管外层,store 管 harness。\n4. `return state` 是否多余?严格说,dict 是引用,原地改 `state[\"harness\"]` 后不 return 也生效。但 return + `state = self._transition(...)` 让每个调用点的数据流显式可见(\"这里 state 变了\"),与 _run 里其他赋值风格一致。这是可读性选择,不是必须。\n5. 另一个真实价值:keyword-only 签名 + terminal_intent 透传。\n\n