eir use case: 根据日志判断当前代码位置 (locate the code position from the log). This is a legitimate logging practice — log statements as landmarks. Like grep-ability: you see \"phase=build_context\" and grep the codebase for that string to find the exact line.\n\nLet me think about which is actually better, honestly:\n\n**My original argument (read from state)**: single source of truth — if transition target changes, log auto-follows; no drift.\n\n**User's counter-argument (hardcode literal)**: \n1. Log = landmark for locating code. Hardcoded string is greppable and unambiguous per site.\n2. \"与当前状态保持一致...要手写的\" — deliberate manual sync: if you change the transition, you must consciously update the log → the mismatch becomes a review checkpoint rather than silent auto-follow. Hmm wait, is manual sync GOOD? My earlier stance: auto-follow prevents drift. Their stance: deliberate coupling forces awareness.\n\nActually there's a subtle point where the user is RIGHT and my approach has a real flaw:\n\nWith `state[\"harness\"][\"phase\"]` read dynamically, if someone changes the transition to a different phase, the log **silently** follows. But consider: the log line's PURPOSE is human observability. If the transition semantics change, does the auto-following log remain correct? Yes — it logs what actually happened. That's the \"truthful observer\" view.\n\nBut the user's view: the log line is also a **code landmark**. `Harness phase=build_context` as a constant string is greppable: grep \"phase=build_context\" → finds the exact call site. With dynamic value, grep \"phase=\" finds 13 sites... well, only the ones that exist. Currently only ONE such log exists (entry). If they later add per-transition logs, each would need distinguishing.\n\nHmm, but actually with hardcoded value, grep finds the site ONLY if the value uniquely identifies the site. BUILD_CONTEXT has two transition sites (entry + next round). If both hardcode \"phase=build_context\", grep finds 2 sites — ambiguity remains. So hardcoding doesn't fully solve site-identification either; it just narrows it.\n\nHowever — there's a cleaner way to identify the call site from logs: the message format itself. E.g., different messages: \"Harness run entering build_context (first round)\" vs \"Harness run rebuilding context\". Or include iteration number. Actually the standard solution for \"locate code from log\": include run_id (already), and use DISTINCT message templates per site. The phase value is about the STATE, not about the SITE.\n\nBut wait — practically, in THIS codebase, is the phase value a good site proxy? Transition targets: BUILD_CONTEXT (×2), PLAN (×2), VALIDATE_ACTION (×1... let me recall _run: entry→BUILD_CONTEXT, ③→PLAN, ⑤→VALIDATE_ACTION, ⑩→EXECUTE_TOOL, ⑪→HANDLE_TOOL_RESULT, ⑬→RECORD_OBSERVATION, ⑭→BUILD_CONTEXT+PLAN; plus _finalize, _pause, resume paths elsewhere). So several collisions. A hardcoded phase string doesn't uniquely identify a site anyway. If they want site identification, the right tool is: distinct log message text per site (which naturally hardcodes the phase in context), OR include the step number from my annotations (①-⑭?), or just rely on %s args.\n\nNow, the \"drift\" debate reconsidered: \n\n- Dynamic (`state[\"harness\"][\"phase\"]`): log always truthful to what happened; site ambiguity; if code changes, log meaning changes silently (but it stays CORRECT).\n- Hardcoded (`LoopPhaseStatusType.BUILD_CONTEXT.value` literal): if code changes and log not updated, log LIES — worse than ambiguous! A lying log is worse than a truthful-but-generic one. BUT the user argues the forced manual update is a feature (you touch the line anyway).\n\nHmm — but honestly, if someone changes the transition target phase, they edit the `_transition(...)` call on the lines directly ABOVE the log. The log literal is 2 lines away. The risk of forgetting is low, and code review would catch it... whereas the dynamic version can never lie. Classic logging wisdom: log what actually happened, not what you planned. `state[\"harness\"][\"phase\"]` = what actually landed in the persisted state. That's the stronger principle for correctness.\n\nBUT the user's need \"判断当前代码位置\" is real. How to satisfy both? \n\nOption: hardcode the phase in the MESSAGE STRING as static text (not via %s):\n```python\nlogger.info(\n    \"Harness entering build_context: run_id=%s turn_id=%s\", ...)\n```\nThis is a per-site template — the text IS the landmark. It can't drift relative to itself (message text and site are one thing; if you change the transition, you change the message because you're editing right there). And grep works.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually let me reconsider: is there anything wrong with hardcoding `\"Harness phase=build_context\"` as message text? The","startTime":1791428023754,"lastModifiedTime":1791428023754}]}],"deviceType":"Local","completedAt":1789989100395},{"id":"f54564da-1721-49b6-b8dd-1ad98bb458eb","summary":"","role":"user","status":"success","content":"不折腾状态了，我们增加一个\"step\": xxx，step是str，直接写死中文汉字","payload":{"query":"不折腾状态了，我们增加一个\"step\": xxx，step是str，直接写死中文汉字","rawMessage":"不折腾状态了，我们增加一个\"step\": xxx，step是str，直接写死中文汉字","model":{"displayName":"Auto","modelId":"auto","requestType":"COMATE_DEFAULT_MODE","mode":"NORMAL"},"agent":{"agentId":1,"agentName":"Comate","agentImage":"zulu"},"metrics":{"model":"Zulu","function":""},"knowledgeList":[{"id":"app/agent/loop_controller/controller.py","name":"controller.py","type":"CURRENT_FILE"}],"isCurrentFileSelected":false,"sendMode":"normal","clientRequestId":"725a9d77-e138-4079-be8b-c82d70e8ca91","requestedAt":1789989144461,"localPermissionMode":"full-access","codex":{"startGoal":false,"startPlan":false,"compact":false},"id":"f54564da-1721-49b6-b8dd-1ad98bb458eb"},"discard":false,"context":{"conversationId":21049217,"taskId":3511606,"capabilitySnapshot":{"version":1,"skillFingerprint":"cc89f1bf6393a278da603fb8c6345718b0afff8a","subAgentsFingerprint":"fd5f5328b64d04b5298af7f280460d92b7f026b2","sent