epository — concrete, no protocol, imports AsyncQdrantClient directly.\n2. Who consumes MetaMetricsSemanticRepository? Does the consumer type it concretely?\n3. Earlier I claimed the project convention was \"Repository 命名\" (which justified the rename). But the user is now showing the deeper convention: NO protocols in the repositories layer at all.\n4. Where DO protocols exist in this codebase? app/agent/context_engine/interfaces.py (a whole file of protocols!), app/agent/loop_controller/contracts.py (ports), app/agent/memory/interfaces.py?, app/agent/tool_runtime/contracts.py... So the agent layer uses protocols heavily (seam design), while the repositories layer is concrete.\n\nLet me verify who consumes MetaMetricsSemanticRepository and with what type annotation. If the consumer also annotates concretely (e.g., `metrics_repo: MetaMetricsSemanticRepository`), then that's the \"concrete all the way\" style — and the user's point stands: the repositories layer is concrete-first by convention; the protocol for ContextStore/ContextRepository is an outlier introduced by the context engine's design.\n\nThe honest answer:\n- 对,你指出的对:仓储层的既有工程全是具象直连,没有一个协议——MetaMetricsSemanticRepository、harness_run_repository 等都是。协议不是这个项目的普遍约定,而是 context_engine 这一个模块的设计选择。\n- 差异的原因:消费方不同。检索仓储的消费方(召回节点)直接 new/传入具象;而 ContextEngine 是\"11 个依赖的编排器\",它的接口文件(interfaces.py)里整个是一组协议——那是这个引擎自带的设计风格(独立可测试、可组装)。\n- So the REAL question: engine 的这套协议风格,在这个\"全具象\"的项目里是不是异类?要不要把 11 个协议全删掉,统一成项目主流的具象风格?\n- 诚实评估:如果统一成具象,engine 的 11 个协议文件(interfaces.py)基本可删,InMemory fakes 保留(鸭子类型照跑,反正没 mypy)。损失:engine 依赖面说明、fake 类型合法性、interfaces.py 整个文件。收益:全项目风格统一,少一个\"为什么 engine 特殊\"的困惑——这正是你这几轮反复困惑的根源!\n- 这个困惑本身是信号:协议风格对现在的你/团队是认知负担。如果团队不接 mypy、不打算多实现,全具象是更一致的选择。代价是 engine 测试 fake 的类型合法性(反正没类型检查,零实际代价)。\n\nWait — but I need to be careful about what the user is actually asking. \"也没有进行要有一个interface的类型做定义呀\" — they're using this as evidence in the ongoing debate: the codebase doesn't do interfaces, so ContextRepository is unnecessary. They're building their case to delete it (or the whole protocols file).\n\nMy response strategy per memory: 先给生产侧论证,然后服从用户裁决. The user has surfaced a REAL inconsistency I should honestly acknowledge: the project is concrete-first; protocols exist only in the agent engine modules (context_engine/interfaces.py, loop_controller/contracts.py, memory/interfaces.py, tool_runtime/contracts.py?). Let me verify the scope of protocol usage quickly before answering:\n\n- grep \"Protocol\" across app/\n- Who consumes MetaMetricsSemanticRepository — concrete typing?\n\nThis will let me give an honest map: \"协议集中在这几个 agent 模块(自带风格),其余全具象\". Then frame the real decision: engine 模块是保留自己的协议风格,还是向项目主流看齐. And honestly: the user's repeated confusion IS a signal that the engine's interface style doesn't pay its way for this team (no mypy, no second implementation planned, small team). My earlier recommendation stands for the reasons given, but the consistency argument is now materially stronger — I should say that honestly.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the whole thing. What is my honest engineering view here?\n\nThe context_engine was likely designed (by me? by the original author?) as a \"clean architecture\" module with full DI via protocols. The rest of the app is pragmatic concrete. Both styles have costs:\n- Engine style: indirection, cognitive overhead (user is experiencing exactly this), files like interfaces.py full of `...`.\n- Pragmatic style: harder to unit test in isolation (but tests use fakes anyway via duck typing — which WORKS without protocols!), tighter coupling.\n\nKey insight: **the tests prove the protocols are not load-bearing for testability** — duck typing works without them (Python). The protocols are pure static-typing documentation. In a project without mypy/pyright enforcement, that documentation is only worth what the team values it.\n\nSo the honest position: for THIS codebase (no static checking, pragmatic concrete style, single developer learning the code), the protocol layer in context_engine is optional documentation that costs comprehension. The user's instinct (delete/simplify) is defensible NOW on consistency grounds — I was defending it mostly on \"type documentation\" grounds which they've shown they don't value (they find it 畸形/confusing).\n\nBut — deleting ALL protocols from engine (11 params) is a big sweep. The immediate question is still ContextRepository. Let me structure:\n\n1. 承认事实:你看得对,仓储层全是具象直连,项目主流确实没有 interface。协议只集中在 agent 的三个引擎模块(context_engine/interfaces.py、loop_controller/contracts.py、memory/…)——那是它们自带的外来风格。\n2. 所以你的困惑是真实的信号:这套协议在\"全具象、无 mypy\"的项目里,是认知负债大于收益的存在。我之前按协议本身的教科书价值辩护,但没把\"项目主流风格\"这个权重算够。\n3. 现在真正的决策不是 ContextRepository 一个,而是:engine 模块的协议风格保不保留。三个选项:\n   a. 保留现状(协议文档价值)\n   b. 只删 ContextRepository,engine 里留 10 个协议(最差——风格更混)\n   c. 全删 interfaces.py,engine/factory/history 签名全部具象化,与项目主流统一;InMemory fakes 保留,运行照跑\n4. 如果按\"项目一致性\"最高优先级,选 c;我建议的方向其实可以诚实地改成:与其纠结一个协议,不如把 engine 模块整体对齐项目风格。但这是 11 个参数的大扫除,影响 engine/history/factory 签名 + 测试构造。\n5. 你拍板,选哪个我执行哪个。\n\nHmm wait — but is (c) actually GOOD advice? Let me think about the downside honestly. If they later add mypy or a second storage, they'd re-add... that's speculative. The user consistently optimizes for: minimal, readable, consistent, no speculative layers. The fakes still work. The engine is 1 module. For a solo/small team project without static checking, concrete-everywhere IS the mainstream Python style (most FastAPI projects do exactly this). The \"clean architecture\" protocol layer is a style import from Java/Go world (which the user knows) — and ironically the user hates it here.\n\nI think honest advice now: the protocols in this codebase pay for themselves only if (a) static checking is enforced, or (b) the team values contract-as-documentation. Neither holds today. The user's consistency argument is legitimate. I'll present option c as the coherent end-state, note the sweep size, and let them decide. Also note: