se":false,"children":[{"id":"960c2672-4028-4210-b72b-00fd4e462d7a","type":"REASON","end":true,"content":"The user is questioning my comment in working.py:\n\n```python\n# ② 尾部窗口截断：limit=None 读全量（ContextEngine 传 None，裁剪留给 token 预算）；否则取最近 limit 条，max_items 是装配期兜底上限。\nrequested_limit = len(messages) if limit is None else max(0, limit)\n```\n\nTheir confusion: \"我没有太理解裁剪留给 token 预算，len(messages)这是token？这样太草率了吧\" — \"I don't understand 'trimming is left to the token budget'. Is len(messages) tokens? That seems too sloppy/careless.\"\n\nThey think `len(messages)` is being used as a token count — a reasonable misreading of my comment. I need to clarify:\n\n1. `len(messages)` is NOT tokens — it's just \"how many messages did the query return\". `requested_limit = len(messages)` means \"no limit requested → keep all of them\". The variable name is about message COUNT, not tokens.\n\n2. Where does the token budget actually trim? The chain:\n   - working.py returns ALL messages as MemoryRecord list (no token awareness at all in this file)\n   - engine.py `_build` ④: `history.prepare(..., token_budget=int(candidate_budget * self.policy.working_history_ratio))` — passes a TOKEN budget to ConversationHistoryManager.prepare\n   - history.py `prepare()` — this is where token-based windowing happens: it keeps recent messages while they fit in the token budget, and summarizes/compresses the older overflow. The items returned are ContextItem with token_count computed via token_counter.\n   - Then selector spends candidate_budget, and ⑨ `_fit_compiled_budget` re-checks real tokens after compile.\n\nSo the answer: working.py's job is deliberately \"fetch everything, count-free\"; the token-aware trimming happens downstream in history.py prepare(). The design rationale: WorkingMemory is a storage-layer reader (SQL), it doesn't own a tokenizer; token decisions belong to the context-engine layer that has token_counter and budget policy.\n\nLet me verify history.py prepare() to describe the